(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-accountable"]={"plain":{"bills":{"accountable":{"slug":"accountable","title":"The Accountable Government Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"This Act may be cited as the **Accountable Government Act**.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** Congress imposes duties upon officers and employees of the executive branch by statute. The performance of a statutory duty does not depend upon the consent of the officer who bears it.\n\n**(2)** A statutory duty is not self-suspending. Where an officer subject to a duty contends that the Constitution excuses its performance, the contention is a claim to be resolved, not a resolution. An arrangement under which the officer's own assertion determines the question leaves the duty enforceable only at the election of the party it binds.\n\n**(3)** An assertion that the Constitution excuses performance is a defense. Like every other defense, it is subject to adjudication, and its validity is determined by a court rather than by the party advancing it.\n\n**(4)** In *Trump v. Cook*, No. 25A312, 609 U.S. ___ (2026), the Supreme Court held that a statutory requirement of cause for removal does not permit a decision to be made \"for any reason, or no reason,\" and that a court must \"independently interpret the statute and effectuate the will of Congress subject to constitutional limits.\" Slip op. 9. The Court further observed that, absent such constraints, \"any perceived or alleged misstep (past or present) could provide a ready pretext for a Governor's removal\u2014a fact that he would surely know, and that would surely weigh on him as he decided what to say and how to vote.\" Slip op. 14\u201315. The same principle governs any statutory requirement that reasons be stated.\n\n**(5)** A requirement to state reasons is satisfied by stating reasons. A citation to a source of authority identifies the power claimed; it does not supply the reason the statute requires.\n\n**(6)** Requiring performance to continue while a constitutional objection is litigated is the ordinary rule of American law. A party subject to a legal obligation complies while seeking relief from it, and obtains a stay from a court if the equities warrant one. This section applies that ordinary rule to statutory duties owed by the executive branch, and preserves the stay.\n\n**(7)** The principle that an agency must act upon contemporaneously stated grounds, and may not supply its reasons afterward, is long established in the law of reasoned decisionmaking. This section applies that settled principle to the particular case of a claim of constitutional excuse, and adopts no standard of adequacy for the reasons stated.\n\n**(8)** The authority of Congress to prescribe the duties of offices it creates, and the manner in which those duties are recorded and reported, is an affirmative power conferred by Article I. It is not a residue remaining after the requirements of Article II have been satisfied.\n\n**(9)** Congress has enacted many duties upon the executive branch to which no consequence attaches, and has found that tightening such a duty without attaching a consequence does not strengthen it. A rule of construction applicable to statutory duties generally supplies what would otherwise require the amendment of each statute separately, and does so without enlarging any duty Congress has enacted.\n\n**(10)** The considerations in paragraphs (1) through (8) apply with equal force to the duties this Act creates, considered alone. Congress would enact this section as to those duties whether or not it may be applied to duties imposed by other Acts.\n\n**(11)** The Constitution distinguishes officers of the United States, who must be appointed in the manner the Appointments Clause prescribes, from employees, who need not be. The distinction is longstanding and is applied by reference to whether a position is continuing and established by law and whether its holder exercises significant authority pursuant to the laws of the United States.\n\n**(12)** That distinction is presently determined case by case, after the fact, and at the expense of the person whose status is in question. An agency is better placed than a court to identify which of its own positions are officer positions, and can do so in advance at negligible cost.\n\n**(13)** This section creates no protection for any officer or employee and confers no tenure. It supplies a definition, a published record, and a rule of severance, so that provisions of this Act may be applied and, if necessary, severed by class rather than defeated entirely.\n\n**(14)** An explanation offered after the fact is not the explanation for which an officer acted. Where an agency has departed from its own established practice and has supplied its reasons only after the action was challenged, those reasons have on examination repeatedly proved to have been constructed for the occasion. **A requirement that the reasons be recorded when the action is taken is therefore not an added formality. It is the only condition under which the reason stated and the reason operative are the same reason.**\n\n**(15)** The limits Congress has placed on the financial conduct of officers and employees of the United States reach officers and employees of the United States, including the special Government employees that section 202(a) of title 18, United States Code, defines, and neither those limits nor that section names an individual who performs the work of the United States under a contract, grant, or other arrangement. At many offices such individuals outnumber the officers and employees beside whom they work, and they hold the same non-public information.\n\n**(16)** The edge that non-public information confers follows the access, not the form of employment. A limit on the use of that access which binds an officer, and does not bind the individual at the next desk who holds the same access under an arrangement, is a limit that can be escaped by changing the form of employment, and \u00a7 0-7A states the limits that follow the access.\n\n**(17)** An individualized adverse action decides a person's status, livelihood, or liberty. The interest a person has in such a decision is not exhausted by its correctness. **It extends to the decision's authorship: a determination made by a person can be explained by that person, attributed to that person, and answered by that person.** A determination issuing from a computational process supplies none of those, however accurate the process and however carefully it was built.\n\n**(18)** This Act does not limit an agency's use of computational tools, and \u00a7 2-2B states what those tools may do in the preparation of a determination. **The question \u00a7 2-2B settles is which participant in the process decides.** A requirement that a natural person decide displaces no tool, and it requires no inquiry into how carefully that person deliberated \u2014 an inquiry no record could support and no officer should conduct. Where the published record shows an unbroken rate of agreement between the deciding person and a system's recommendation, that fact is published and left to speak for itself."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"Terms used in this Act are defined in the Section in which they are used. Where a term is defined in more than one Section, the definition in the Section in which the term appears governs its use in that Section."},{"anchor":"sec-0-4","heading":"\u00a7 0-4. Assertion of constitutional authority","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"**(a) Definitions.** In this section \u2014\n\n**(1) \"Covered official\"** means an officer or employee of the executive branch of the United States, other than the President. The term includes a person serving in an acting, temporary, or delegated capacity.\n\n**(2) \"Covered duty\"** means a duty, condition, or requirement imposed upon a covered official by this Act or by any other Act of Congress, and owed to Congress, to a court, or to the public, to \u2014\n\n**(A)** state, record, or preserve the reasons for an action;\n\n**(B)** create, preserve, transmit, or publish a record, report, or determination;\n\n**(C)** provide notice or afford an opportunity to be heard; or\n\n**(D)** take or refrain from an action as a condition of the effectiveness of another action.\n\nThe term does not include a duty owed solely to another component of the executive branch, or a duty concerning the internal management of an agency.\n\n**(3) \"Constitutional assertion\"** means a claim, however phrased and in whatever form advanced, that a provision of the Constitution excuses, displaces, suspends, or renders unenforceable a covered duty, or that it deprives a tribunal of authority to consider whether a covered duty was performed.\n\n**(4) \"Statement of reasons\"** means a contemporaneous written statement of the factual and legal grounds for an action, signed by the covered official who took it.\n\n**(b) An assertion is not a statement of reasons.** A constitutional assertion does not constitute a statement of reasons and does not satisfy, in whole or in part, any requirement that a covered official state, record, publish, or transmit the reasons for an action.\n\n**(c) An assertion does not suspend performance.** A covered duty continues in effect, and remains enforceable according to its terms, notwithstanding a constitutional assertion, unless and until \u2014\n\n**(1)** a court of competent jurisdiction holds the covered duty invalid or inapplicable as to the covered official; or\n\n**(2)** a court of competent jurisdiction stays the operation of the covered duty pending adjudication.\n\n**(d) An assertion does not defeat jurisdiction.** A constitutional assertion does not deprive any court, board, or other tribunal of jurisdiction that it otherwise possesses to determine whether a covered duty applied, whether it was performed, and what consequences this Act or any other law attaches to its non-performance. Such a tribunal retains jurisdiction to adjudicate the constitutional assertion itself.\n\n**(e) Conditions upon making an assertion.** A constitutional assertion has no effect for any purpose unless \u2014\n\n**(1)** it is made in writing and signed by the head of the department or agency concerned, who may not delegate this function;\n\n**(2)** it identifies the specific provision of the Constitution relied upon and the specific covered duty said to be excused;\n\n**(3)** it states the grounds upon which the covered official contends the provision applies to that duty;\n\n**(4)** it is made not later than the time at which performance of the covered duty was due, or, where performance was due before the assertion could reasonably have been formulated, not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` thereafter; and\n\n**(5)** it is published and transmitted to the Committees on the Judiciary of the Senate and the House of Representatives not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` after it is made.\n\n**(6) Classified and statutorily protected material.** Where an assertion cannot be published in full without disclosing properly classified information or information whose disclosure another statute prohibits, the covered official shall transmit the assertion in full to the Committees on the Judiciary by a means appropriate to its protection, and shall publish an unclassified statement identifying the constitutional provision relied upon and the covered duty said to be excused. The requirement to make the assertion is not excused. **Nothing in this paragraph authorizes review of a classification decision or of a determination that information is protected from disclosure by another statute.**\n\n**(7) Lapse.** Not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` after making a constitutional assertion, the head of the department or agency concerned shall commence an action for declaratory relief as to the validity of the assertion, or shall withdraw it. An assertion neither so pursued nor withdrawn lapses, and thereafter has no effect for any purpose.\n\n**(f) Effect of non-performance.**\n\n**(1) Duties to state reasons or to create, preserve, transmit, or publish a record.** Where a covered official does not perform such a covered duty, the duty is not satisfied and continues to run, and every consequence that this Act or any other law attaches to its non-performance attaches without regard to the constitutional assertion.\n\n**(2) Actions conditioned upon performance.** Where a statute conditions the effectiveness of an action upon the performance of a covered duty, the action takes effect upon performance of that duty and not before. The making of a constitutional assertion does not advance the time at which the action takes effect.\n\n**(3) Time and manner of raising.** The effectiveness of an action under paragraph (2) may be determined by any tribunal in any proceeding in which the validity or effect of that action is directly at issue, and in which the question is raised not later than `[SCHEDULE: period]` after the action was taken or after the omission became known or reasonably should have become known, whichever is later.\n\n**(4) Action to compel publication.** A person to whom a covered official has failed to publish or transmit an assertion as required by subsection (e)(5) may bring a civil action in the district courts of the United States to compel publication or transmission. Relief in such an action is limited to publication or transmission and to costs and reasonable attorney's fees. **No such action lies as to the merits of the constitutional assertion, as to the performance of the underlying covered duty, or as to any other provision of this section.**\n\n**(g) Rules of construction.**\n\n**(1) The defense is preserved.** Nothing in this section limits the authority of a covered official to advance, or of a court to adjudicate, a claim that a provision of the Constitution excuses performance of a covered duty. This section governs the sequence in which such a claim is resolved and the effect of the claim before it is resolved; it does not govern its merits.\n\n**(2) No inference.** Compliance with subsection (e) shall not be taken as evidence that a constitutional assertion is valid, and non-compliance shall not be taken as evidence that it is invalid.\n\n**(3) The President.** Nothing in this section imposes any duty upon the President or subjects any action of the President to any requirement or consequence established by this section.\n\n**(4) Stay preserved.** Nothing in this section limits the authority of a court to stay the operation of a covered duty, or to grant any other relief, upon such terms as the court considers just.\n\n**(5) No jurisdiction conferred.** Except as provided in subsection (f)(4), this section confers no jurisdiction upon any tribunal and enlarges no tribunal's authority. It provides only that a constitutional assertion does not subtract from jurisdiction that a tribunal otherwise possesses.\n\n**(6) Statutory authority unaffected.** Nothing in this section limits the exercise of authority expressly conferred by statute, including authority to waive, suspend, modify, or decline to apply a statutory requirement. This section applies only to a constitutional assertion.\n\n**(7) No other cause of action created.** Except as provided in subsection (f)(4), this section creates no right of action and no remedy not otherwise available.\n\n**(8) An excuse removed, not a duty enlarged.** This section imposes no duty that another Act of Congress has not already imposed, and does not alter the scope, content, or addressee of any such duty. Its operation is to remove an excuse for non-performance, and consequences attach under this section only as another law already provides.\n\n**(9) Application.** This section applies to a constitutional assertion made on or after the effective date of this Act. **Reliance, on or after that date, upon an assertion made before that date is itself a constitutional assertion for purposes of this section, and subsection (e) applies to it as of the date of reliance.** Nothing in this section declares any past conduct unlawful or attaches any consequence to conduct occurring before the effective date.\n\n**(h) Protection of the official who performs.**\n\n**(1) Prohibition.** No adverse action may be taken against a covered official because the official performed a covered duty, declined to make or to join in a constitutional assertion, or declined to sign an assertion under subsection (e)(1).\n\n**(2) Employees who are not officers.** As to a covered official who is not an officer of the United States within the meaning of the Appointments Clause, an adverse action taken in violation of paragraph (1) is void, and the official's pay, status, and duties continue by operation of law.\n\n**(3) Officers.** As to a covered official who is an officer of the United States, an adverse action taken within `[SCHEDULE: days]` after the official performed a covered duty or declined to make or sign an assertion is effective only upon a written statement of the grounds for the action, signed by the officer taking it, published, and transmitted to the Committees on the Judiciary of the Senate and the House of Representatives.\n\n**(4) No tenure conferred.** Nothing in this subsection confers tenure in office, limits removal upon grounds unrelated to the conduct described in paragraph (1), or requires that any officer be reinstated.\n\n**(i) Severability.** This section is severable from every other provision of this Act, and each subsection of this section is severable from every other. If any provision of this section, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this section and its application to other persons and circumstances shall not be affected, and the remainder of this Act shall continue in effect. Congress would have enacted this section, and each of its subsections, independently of any provision or application held invalid \u2014 including as applied solely to duties imposed by this Act, as recited in \u00a7 0-2(10)."},{"anchor":"sec-0-5","heading":"\u00a7 0-5. The function performed determines the legal regime","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"**(a) General rule.** Where an officer, employee, or member of the armed forces of the United States performs a function described in subsection (b), **the law governing that function governs the conduct** \u2014 without regard to the department or component that employs the person, the platform from which the function is performed, or the designation the executive applies to the operation.\n\n**(b) Law-enforcement functions.** The following are law-enforcement functions for purposes of this Section: the interdiction of contraband; the search or seizure of a vessel, aircraft, vehicle, structure, or person; the arrest or apprehension of a person for prosecution; the seizure of property for forfeiture; and **the use of force in the course of any of them.**\n\n**(c) A designation does not change the rule.** A designation of an organization, a person, or a class of persons \u2014 including a designation as a foreign terrorist organization \u2014 **does not of itself create an armed conflict, confer combatant status, or displace the law otherwise governing a function described in subsection (b).**\n\n**(d) Who performs the function.** Where a function described in subsection (b) is performed from a military platform or in the course of a military operation, **the search, seizure, arrest, and use-of-force functions shall be performed by personnel holding law-enforcement authority**, and the rules governing that authority apply to their performance.\n\n**(e) No immunity by characterization.** **No immunity, privilege, or defense available to a combatant attaches to a person by reason of performing a function described in subsection (b)**, and the characterization of an operation does not supply one.\n\n**(f) What this Section does not determine.** **This Section does not determine whether an armed conflict exists.** It does not apply to a function performed in the course of hostilities authorized by a declaration of war or by specific statutory authorization; it does not limit the President's authority as Commander in Chief to direct the armed forces, to determine the disposition of forces, or to respond to an attack; and it does not bear on the law of armed conflict or on any obligation of the United States under a treaty. **Where the Congress has authorized hostilities, this Section does not reach conduct within them** \u2014 but an authorization reaches only the hostilities it authorizes, and **a designation made by the executive under any other authority does not bring a person or an operation within one.**\n\n**(g) The domestic analogue.** Section 1385 of title 18, United States Code, restricts the use of the armed forces \"to execute the laws\" except as expressly authorized. **This Section states, as to the functions described in subsection (b), a rule of the same character that operates without regard to where the function is performed** \u2014 and it neither construes nor limits that section.\n\n**(h) Anti-substitution.** Where an authority other than one governing a function described in subsection (b) is invoked to accomplish substantially the same result, **\u00a7 9-3 applies according to its terms.**\n\n**(i) Severability.** This Section is severable from every other provision of this Act, and each subsection is severable from every other. **If subsection (d) is held invalid, subsections (a) through (c) and (e) remain in force.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. Officers and employees","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"**(a) Definitions.** In this section \u2014\n\n**(1) \"Officer of the United States\"** means a covered official who occupies a continuing position established by law and who exercises significant authority pursuant to the laws of the United States.\n\n**(2) \"Employee\"** means a covered official who is not an officer of the United States.\n\n**(b) Register of officer positions.**\n\n**(1) Designation.** Not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` after the effective date of this Act, and thereafter not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` after establishing or materially altering a position, the head of each agency shall designate each position in the agency that is an officer position.\n\n**(2) Publication.** The head of each agency shall publish the designations and transmit them to the Committees on the Judiciary of the Senate and the House of Representatives. Each entry shall state the title of the position, the appointing authority relied upon, and the date of designation.\n\n**(3) Default.** A position that has not been designated and published as an officer position is treated as an employee position for all purposes of this Act.\n\n**(c) Effect of designation.**\n\n**(1) Controlling.** A designation published under subsection (b) controls for all purposes of this Act.\n\n**(2) Burden.** A party contending that a position not so designated is nonetheless an officer position bears the burden of establishing it.\n\n**(3) No redesignation during a proceeding.** A designation made or altered after an adverse action has been taken with respect to a position, or after a proceeding concerning that position has commenced, has no effect in that proceeding.\n\n**(4) Designation is not appointment.** A designation neither effects an appointment nor validates or cures any defect in one. Designation of a position as an officer position is an acknowledgment that the position must be filled in conformity with the Appointments Clause.\n\n**(d) Rules of construction.**\n\n**(1) No tenure conferred.** Nothing in this Act confers tenure in office upon any officer of the United States or limits the authority of the President to remove an officer.\n\n**(2) No protection created.** This section establishes no protection for any covered official. Its operation is confined to definition, publication, and severance.\n\n**(3) Source of authority.** Provisions of this Act applicable to employees rest upon the authority of Congress to establish offices, to prescribe the conditions of federal employment, and to provide for the execution of its enumerated powers.\n\n**(4) No position taken on the reach of removal doctrine.** Nothing in this section states, assumes, or depends upon any proposition concerning the extent to which the removal authority recognized in the decisions of the Supreme Court applies to employees who are not officers of the United States.\n\n**(e) Severance by class.** Where a provision of this Act is held invalid as applied to officers of the United States, that provision continues in effect as applied to employees. Where a provision is held invalid as applied to a class of officers, it continues in effect as applied to all other covered officials. Congress would have enacted each such provision as applied to employees alone.\n\n**(f) Action to compel publication.** A person may bring a civil action in the district courts of the United States to compel publication or transmission required by subsection (b)(2). Relief is limited to publication or transmission and to costs and reasonable attorney's fees. No such action lies as to the correctness of a designation or as to any other provision of this section."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Limits follow the function","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"**(a) General rule.** A person who performs a governmental function under a contract, grant, cooperative agreement, intergovernmental agreement, detail, or other arrangement with the United States **is subject to every limit, duty, condition, and prohibition that would bind an officer or employee of the United States performing that function.**\n\n**(b) The rule is keyed to the limit, not to the conduct.** It is not a defense that the conduct performed would be lawful if performed by a private person for a private purpose, or that no statute names the contractor. **The question is what limit binds the function**, and that limit binds whoever performs it.\n\n**(c) Omission from the instrument is not a defense.** Subsection (a) applies **whether or not the instrument mentions it**, and neither the omission of a term, nor any provision allocating risk, nor any term purporting to limit liability, affects its application. **A limit that applies by force of this Section applies as though set out in the instrument.**\n\n**(d) Governmental functions.** A governmental function includes: holding a person in custody; questioning a person in custody or under compulsion; conducting a search, seizure, arrest, stop, or surveillance; using force; screening, vetting, or adjudicating an application, claim, or status; collecting, analysing, or matching information about identified persons on behalf of the United States; transporting a person in custody; and providing medical or mental-health care to a person in custody.\n\n**(e) Residual.** A governmental function also includes **any other function in which the person exercises authority over another person on behalf of the United States, or performs a function that a statute or regulation assigns to an officer or employee.** The enumeration in subsection (d) is not exhaustive, and **the omission of a function from it is not a determination that the function is outside this Section.**\n\n**(f) What is not a governmental function.** The supply of goods, materials, construction, or services that do not involve the exercise of authority over a person **is not within this Section**, and nothing in this Section subjects an ordinary vendor of the United States to the duties of an officer.\n\n**(g) Publication.** Each agency shall publish every instrument under which a governmental function is performed by a person other than an officer or employee, and each amendment to one, withholding only such portions as a statute prohibits from disclosure, **with an index of what is withheld and the statutory basis.**\n\n**(h) No indemnification for the worst conduct.** No agency shall indemnify a person for a punitive award, or for conduct a trier of fact found to have been undertaken with knowledge of its unlawfulness, arising from the performance of a governmental function. **A provision of any instrument to the contrary is void.**\n\n**(i) Consent to suit.** An instrument under which a governmental function is performed shall provide that the person **consents to suit in the district courts of the United States** for a breach of a limit applied by this Section, and **waives any defense that it is not a governmental entity, is not a State actor, or does not act under color of law or authority.** \u00a7 0-7(c) applies to the omission of this term.\n\n**(j) Relation to other provisions.** This Section states generally what \u00a7 8-7 of the Enforceable Rights Act states for custody and what \u00a7 101(c) of the Data Warrant Act states for the acquisition of records. **Where either applies, it applies according to its own terms**, and this Section neither narrows it nor is limited by it.\n\n**(k) Severability.** This Section is severable from every other provision of this Act. **If subsection (e) is held invalid, subsections (a) through (d) remain in force** as to every function enumerated."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7a","heading":"\u00a7 0-7A. Limits follow access to non-public information","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS, RULES OF CONSTRUCTION","body":"**(a) Definitions.** In this Section \u2014\n\n**(1) \"Arrangement with the United States\"** means a contract, a subcontract at any tier, a grant, a cooperative agreement, an other-transaction agreement, an intergovernmental or interagency agreement, a detail, a fellowship, or any other arrangement, however denominated, under which an individual performs services or other work for, on behalf of, or with funds provided by an agency of the executive branch of the United States, whether the individual performs them as a party to the arrangement or as an owner, officer, employee, member, partner, or agent of a party. The forms enumerated in this paragraph are illustrative, and an arrangement of any other form under which services are so performed is an arrangement with the United States regardless of its title or characterization.\n\n**(2) \"Non-public information\"** means information held by the United States or by an agency, office, court, or instrumentality of the United States that has not been made generally available to the public, and includes classified information, information the disclosure of which a statute or regulation restricts, information held in a non-public information system, and information concerning a pending or contemplated action, decision, procurement, investigation, adjudication, or proceeding.\n\n**(3) \"Access individual\"** means an individual who has access to non-public information in the performance of an arrangement with the United States, during the period of that access. An individual has such access during any period in which non-public information is made available to the individual in the performance of the arrangement, and has it, without regard to any other fact, during any period in which the individual holds, by reason of the arrangement, a security clearance issued by the United States or an account on a non-public information system of the United States. The term does not include an officer or employee of the United States, who is subject in that capacity to the law governing officers and employees, and does not include an individual whose performance of an arrangement consists only of the supply of goods or of services or other work performed without access to non-public information. The exclusions of this paragraph are those and only those stated in it.\n\n**(4) \"Party\"** means a person, other than the United States, that is a party to an arrangement with the United States. A party is not an access individual by reason of an individual's access, and this Section imposes no duty on a party other than the duties stated in subsections (c)(6), (e)(3), and (g).\n\n**(5) \"Particular matter\"** includes an investigation, application, request for a ruling or determination, rulemaking, contract, controversy, claim, charge, accusation, arrest, and judicial or other proceeding.\n\n**(6) \"Participate personally and substantially\"** means to participate personally and substantially, through decision, approval, disapproval, recommendation, the rendering of advice, investigation, or otherwise.\n\n**(7) \"Financial interest\"** means, with respect to a particular matter, a financial interest of the access individual; of the individual's spouse; of a dependent child of the individual, meaning a child who is a dependent of the individual within the meaning of section 152 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986; of any other member of the individual's household, meaning an individual who shares the access individual's principal residence as a member of one household; of a general partner of the individual; of an organization in which the individual serves as an officer, director, trustee, general partner, or employee; of a person or organization with which the individual is negotiating or has an arrangement concerning prospective employment; or of a trust or entity that the individual or the individual's spouse settled, derives economic benefit from, or directs the investment of.\n\n**(8) \"Gift\"** has the meaning given in the regulations issued under section 7353 of title 5, United States Code, as in effect on the date of enactment of this Act, and the exclusions and exceptions stated in those regulations on that date apply to an access individual as they apply to an employee. A determination or authorization those regulations assign to an agency designee is made, for an access individual, by the agency under whose arrangement the access exists.\n\n**(9) \"Represent\"** means knowingly to make, with the intent to influence, a communication to or appearance before an officer or employee of a department, agency, court, or court-martial of the United States, on behalf of a person other than the United States, in connection with a particular matter in which the United States is a party or has a direct and substantial interest.\n\n**(10) \"Agency\"** means an agency of the executive branch of the United States, and includes the Executive Office of the President and any component of it to the extent it is a party to an arrangement with the United States.\n\n**(b) The rule.** An access individual is subject to the limits stated in subsections (c) through (f), and to no other limit by reason of this Section. The question under this Section is whether the individual has access, which is determined from the arrangement and from the clearance or account the individual holds, and not whether the function the individual performs is governmental. Where an access individual also performs a governmental function, \u00a7 0-7 applies according to its own terms, and neither Section narrows the other.\n\n**(c) Financial conflict.**\n\n**(1) Prohibition.** An access individual shall not participate personally and substantially, in the performance of an arrangement with the United States, in a particular matter in which, to the individual's knowledge, the individual has a financial interest.\n\n**(2) Exemptions fixed at enactment.** Paragraph (1) does not apply to a financial interest that the regulations issued under section 208(b)(2) of title 18, United States Code, as in effect on the date of enactment of this Act, exempt as too remote or too inconsequential to affect the integrity of an employee's services. Those exemptions apply to an access individual as they apply to an employee, and no regulation issued after that date enlarges or narrows them for purposes of this Section.\n\n**(3) Disclosure and waiver.** Before participating personally and substantially in a particular matter, an access individual shall disclose in writing, to the party through which the individual performs the arrangement and to the agency, each financial interest the individual has in the matter. A disclosure that does not identify the matter and the nature of the interest does not satisfy this paragraph. The agency may waive paragraph (1) as to a disclosed interest by a writing, signed by the head of the agency or by an officer the head has designated in writing for the purpose, that identifies the individual, the matter, and the nature of the interest, states the reason the interest is not so substantial as to be likely to affect the integrity of the individual's services, and is published under subsection (i) before the participation begins. A writing that does not identify the individual, the matter, and the nature of the interest, or that purports to waive paragraph (1) as to a class of individuals, a class of matters, or a class of interests, is not a waiver.\n\n**(4) Consequence for reliance.** A determination, recommendation, evaluation, analysis, or other deliverable prepared with the participation of an access individual in violation of paragraph (1) is not available to the United States, to an agency, or to any officer or employee, in any proceeding, in support of an action, as evidence, or as a basis for deference to any finding it contains. This paragraph does not make an action unlawful, does not create a cause of action, and does not bear on whether the deliverable is correct. A court applying it decides only whether an access individual having a financial interest in the matter participated personally and substantially in preparing the deliverable, whether the interest was exempt under paragraph (2), and whether a waiver satisfying paragraph (3) was published before the participation.\n\n**(5) Disgorgement.** An access individual who violates paragraph (1) is liable to the United States for the gain realized by reason of the participation or, if greater, the increase in the fair market value of the financial interest between the beginning and the end of the participation, and an additional amount equal to that sum, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General. Liability under this paragraph does not require a showing that the participation affected any action of the agency or the outcome of the matter, and no other causal standard applies.\n\n**(6) Removal from the matter.** Upon a disclosure under paragraph (3) that is not waived, and upon learning of a financial interest that was not disclosed, the party shall remove the individual from participation in the matter. A failure to do so is a breach of the arrangement by the party.\n\n**(d) Gifts.**\n\n**(1) Prohibition.** An access individual shall not solicit or accept a gift from a person who is seeking action by, is doing or seeking to do business with, or conducts activities regulated by, the agency function the individual performs under the arrangement, or whose interests may be substantially affected by the performance of that function, or from an organization a majority of whose members are such persons, or a gift given because of the arrangement.\n\n**(2) Consequence.** An access individual who violates paragraph (1) is liable to the United States for the value of the gift, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General, and the violation is a basis for removal of the individual from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords.\n\n**(e) Non-public information.**\n\n**(1) Prohibition.** An access individual shall not use, or allow another to use, non-public information obtained in the performance of an arrangement with the United States, which the individual knows or reasonably should know has not been made generally available to the public, to further a private interest of the individual or of any other person.\n\n**(2) Authorized disclosures.** Paragraph (1) does not reach a communication made in the performance of the arrangement; a disclosure required by law, or made in a judicial, legislative, or administrative proceeding; a disclosure to an agency, to an Inspector General, to the Congress or a committee or Member of Congress acting in an official capacity, or to a law-enforcement authority; or a disclosure protected by a whistleblower statute. The enumeration of this paragraph is exclusive, and the omission of any other class of disclosure is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(3) The party.** A party that directed or requested an access individual to use non-public information in violation of paragraph (1), or that knowingly received such information from an access individual and used it to further its own interest, is liable to the United States for the gain it realized, and an additional amount equal to that gain, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General. A party is not liable under this paragraph by reason of an individual's violation that it did not direct, request, or knowingly receive and use.\n\n**(4) Consequence for the individual.** An access individual who violates paragraph (1) is liable to the United States for the gain realized by the individual or by the person the individual allowed to use the information, and an additional amount equal to that gain, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General, and the violation is a basis for removal of the individual from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords.\n\n**(f) Post-engagement representation.**\n\n**(1) Prohibition.** An individual who, as an access individual, participated personally and substantially in a particular matter that involved a specific party or specific parties at the time of the participation shall not, after the individual's access has ended, represent any other person in connection with that matter.\n\n**(2) Exceptions fixed at enactment.** The exceptions stated in section 207(j) of title 18, United States Code, as in effect on the date of enactment of this Act, apply to paragraph (1) as they apply to section 207(a)(1) of that title, and as they applied on that date to a former officer or employee.\n\n**(3) Consequence.** An individual who violates paragraph (1) is liable to the United States for the compensation received for the representation, and an additional amount equal to that compensation, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General. The representation is not void, and no action taken by an agency or court in the matter is affected by reason of it.\n\n**(g) The arrangement.**\n\n**(1) Term by force of this Section.** The limits stated in subsections (c) through (f) are terms of every arrangement with the United States under which an access individual has access, whether or not the arrangement states them, and neither the omission of a term, nor any provision allocating risk, nor any term purporting to limit liability, affects their application. \u00a7 0-7(c) and \u00a7 0-7(i) apply to such an arrangement as they apply to an instrument under which a governmental function is performed.\n\n**(2) Notice and record.** Each party to such an arrangement shall give each access individual written notice of this Section before the individual's access begins or, for an individual having access on the date this Section first applies to the individual, within 30 days of that date, and shall retain a record of the notice for the period during which an action under this Section could be brought. A notice that does not identify this Section, state the limits of subsections (c) through (f), and state the disclosure duty of subsection (c)(3) does not satisfy this paragraph. A failure to give notice is a breach of the arrangement by the party and excuses no individual.\n\n**(3) Knowledge of the access.** As to an access individual who holds neither a clearance nor an account described in subsection (a)(3), liability under subsections (c)(5), (d)(2), (e)(4), and (f)(3) requires that the individual had received the notice paragraph (2) requires, or otherwise knew of the access, before the conduct; subsection (c)(4) applies without regard to this paragraph.\n\n**(h) Extraterritorial application.** This Section applies to an access individual without regard to the individual's nationality or to the place at which the arrangement is performed, and applies to conduct described in subsections (c) through (f) wherever it occurs.\n\n**(i) Publication.**\n\n**(1) Waivers.** Each agency shall publish each waiver made under subsection (c)(3), and shall maintain a public index of the waivers it has published, identifying for each the individual, the matter, the nature of the interest, and the date. A waiver is published for purposes of subsection (c)(3) only when it appears in the index.\n\n**(2) Counts.** Each agency shall publish annually the number of arrangements with the United States to which it is a party under which an individual holds, by reason of the arrangement, a security clearance issued by the United States or an account on a non-public information system of the agency, the number of such individuals, the number of disclosures received under subsection (c)(3), and the number of waivers made.\n\n**(3) Ministerial.** The duties imposed by this subsection are ministerial and enforceable as provided in \u00a7 1-4, in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.\n\n**(j) Enforcement.**\n\n**(1) Self-executing.** The consequence stated in subsection (c)(4) operates without any action by any officer or court.\n\n**(2) Civil only.** The remedies under this Section are those stated in it. No criminal liability arises under this Act from a violation of this Section, and nothing in this Section limits any criminal provision of title 18, United States Code, as applied to a person it reaches.\n\n**(3) No new body.** No office, board, or commission is created by this Section, and no duty or consequence provided in it depends on the existence, funding, or staffing of any body.\n\n**(4) No rulemaking over the definitions.** No regulation narrows or enlarges the definitions in subsection (a), the exemptions fixed by subsection (c)(2), or the exceptions fixed by subsection (f)(2), for purposes of this Section.\n\n**(5) Limitations.** An action under this Section may be commenced within six years after the later of the conduct or its discovery by the United States, notwithstanding section 2462 of title 28, United States Code.\n\n**(6) Nondeductibility.** An amount paid or recovered under this Section is not deductible for any purpose under the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and no such amount is treated as restitution, remediation, or an amount paid to come into compliance with law for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of that Code.\n\n**(7) Forum.** An action by the United States under this Section is brought in the district courts of the United States. It is not a claim relating to a contract for purposes of chapter 71 of title 41, United States Code, no procedure under that chapter is a prerequisite to it, and nothing in that chapter limits the jurisdiction of the district courts over it. This paragraph does not affect any claim the United States or a party may bring under that chapter for a breach of the arrangement.\n\n**(k) Relation to other law.** This Section is in addition to, and not in lieu of, sections 201 through 209 of title 18, United States Code, as applied to any person they reach; section 2303 of title 41, United States Code, and the regulations issued under it; the Preventing Organizational Conflicts of Interest in Federal Acquisition Act (Public Law 117-324; 41 U.S.C. \u00a7 2303 note) and the regulations issued under it; sections 2102 through 2104 of title 41, United States Code; section 4c(a)(3) and (4) of the Commodity Exchange Act (7 U.S.C. \u00a7 6c(a)(3) and (4)); section 552a(m) of title 5, United States Code; the Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 3-3B and \u00a7 3-3C; and every other prohibition or remedy, and nothing in this Section limits any of them. \u00a7 0-7(f) is not narrowed by this Section, and nothing in this Section subjects a supplier of goods to the United States to any duty by reason of the supply.\n\n**(l) Application in time.** This Section applies to an individual having access on the date of enactment of this Act beginning one year after that date, and to an individual whose access begins after the date of enactment beginning on the later of the date the access begins and one year after the date of enactment. Subsection (f) applies only to participation occurring after this Section applies to the individual. Nothing in this Section declares any past conduct unlawful or attaches any consequence to conduct occurring before the date on which it applies to the individual.\n\n**(m) Severability.** This Section is severable from every other provision of this Act, and each subsection of this Section is severable from every other. The application of this Section to an individual performing an arrangement of any one form enumerated in subsection (a)(1) is severable from its application to every other form. Subsection (c)(4) is severable from subsections (c)(5) and (c)(6), and each of them remains in force if another is held invalid. Subsection (e)(3) is severable from every other provision of subsection (e). If the application of this Section to an individual located outside the United States, or to an individual who is not a national of the United States, is held invalid, the Section remains in force as to every other individual."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Covered authority.** A **statutory** authority of the United States to take an action described in \u00a7 2-1(a) against a person. The term does **not** include any authority conferred directly by Article II of the Constitution, and nothing in this Act applies to the exercise of the pardon power, the conduct of diplomatic relations, the nomination of officers, or the President's authority as Commander in Chief.\n\n**(b) Agency.** Includes the Executive Office of the President and any component thereof to the extent it exercises a covered authority.\n\n**(c) Class of case.** A category of individualized adverse action as defined by the standard published under \u00a7 1-2. An agency may not define a class other than by publication under that Section.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Publication of the applicable standard","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Duty.** Before exercising a covered authority in a class of case, an agency shall publish the **legal standard it applies** to that class, stating the statutory provision relied upon, the findings the agency understands the provision to require, and the evidentiary threshold the agency applies.\n\n**(b) The agency states its own standard.** Nothing in this Section limits what standard an agency may state, requires any particular standard, or authorizes any officer or court to prescribe one.\n\n**(c) Amendment.** An agency may amend a published standard **prospectively**. An amendment applies only to actions initiated after publication, and the agency shall publish the prior standard, the amended standard, and the date of amendment.\n\n**(d) One direction only.** A published standard, training material, manual, or policy document is evidence of what the agency understood the law to require. **It is not evidence that the agency's practice conformed to it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Publication of invocation counts","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Duty.** Each agency shall publish annually, for each covered authority the agency administers, the **number of times the authority was invoked** in the preceding year, disaggregated by class of case and by component, together with the corresponding counts for each of the preceding ten years to the extent the agency's records permit.\n\n**(b) No limitation.** This Section limits no authority, requires no justification, and confers no power on any officer to approve, disapprove, or review any invocation.\n\n**(c) Quarterly reporting on exceedance.** Where invocations within any quarter exceed the reference rate under \u00a7 2-1(d) for a full year, the agency shall publish that fact **within 30 days of the end of the quarter**, without waiting for the annual publication.\n\n**(d) Reconstruction.** Where an agency's records do not permit publication of the counts for a year preceding enactment, the agency shall publish, within **two years** of enactment, the best available reconstruction, and shall state the method used."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3a","heading":"\u00a7 1-3A. Publication of the supervisory chain","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Duty.** For each office or component exercising **investigative or charging authority**, the agency shall publish \u2014 the officer or office to which it reports; the officer or office from which it may receive direction as to a particular matter; and **the instrument that establishes each.**\n\n**(b) Changes published.** A change to anything published under subsection (a) shall be published **within 15 days**, together with **the date of the change and the officer who made it.**\n\n**(c) Anti-relocation.** Where a function is subject to a supervisory chain published under subsection (a), and the function is **transferred to an office not subject to that chain**, the agency shall publish a statement identifying the function, the office from which and to which it moved, **and the difference between the supervisory chains of each.**\n\n**(c-1) Structure, not substance.** This Section requires publication of **the structure of supervision**. It requires no disclosure of any direction given, advice rendered, or communication made in any particular matter, and no privilege is affected.\n\n**(d) It constrains nothing.** This Section **limits no authority**, requires no particular reporting structure, and confers on no officer any power to approve, disapprove, or review a reorganization. **It requires that the architecture be visible**, and nothing else."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Enforcement of this Section","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Ministerial.** The duties imposed by \u00a7\u00a7 1-2, 1-3, and 1-3A are **ministerial** and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by **any person**, without regard to whether the person has been subjected to an action under the authority.\n\n**(b) Informational injury.** A person denied information this Section requires to be published **has suffered injury sufficient to support that action.**\n\n**(c) Fees.** The court shall award reasonable attorney's fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff.\n\n**(d) No new body.** No office, board, or commission is created by this Section, and no duty or consequence provided in it depends on the existence, funding, or staffing of any body."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Somebody reads it","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE RECORD OF PRACTICE","body":"**(a) Duty.** The Inspector General of each agency shall examine, on a sample basis and not less often than annually, the counts published under \u00a7 1-3, the reconstruction published under \u00a7 1-3(d), and a sample of the determinations required by \u00a7 2-2, and shall report whether they are accurate and complete.\n\n**(b) Reported where reports already go.** The examination required by subsection (a) is included in the semiannual report the Inspector General already submits, and no separate report is required.\n\n**(c) Sample only.** Nothing in this Section requires review of every determination, and the Inspector General may set the sample.\n\n**(d) No approval function.** This Section confers no authority to approve, disapprove, delay, or review the merits of any action, and an Inspector General's finding under this Section does not affect the validity of any action.\n\n**(e) Where an agency has no Inspector General.** The examination is performed by the Inspector General designated by the Council of the Inspectors General on Integrity and Efficiency.\n\n**(f) Published.** The examination and its findings are published with the report."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) Individualized adverse action.** An action taken under a covered authority that (1) names or identifies a person or a determinate group of persons, and (2) revokes, suspends, denies, conditions, or terminates a status, license, clearance, benefit, contract, registration, or authorization, or imposes a penalty, detention, exclusion, debarment, or removal.\n\n**(b) Ordinary practice.** With respect to a covered authority and a class of case, **the documented pattern of the agency's prior exercises of that authority**, as established by the counts published under \u00a7 1-3 and the agency's records. Ordinary practice is a **question of historical fact** and is not established by a statement of policy, a training document, or a litigation position.\n\n**(c) Departure.** An individualized adverse action departs from ordinary practice if \u2014\n1. the covered authority is invoked in a **class of case** in which the agency has not invoked it within the preceding ten years;\n2. the covered authority is invoked at an annual rate more than **five times the reference rate**, or more than **three times the reference rate** where the reference rate exceeds ten invocations per year; or\n3. it is taken against a person where the agency has, within the preceding three years, declined to invoke the authority against a person the agency's own records identify as similarly situated in the respects the published standard makes material.\n\n**(d) Reference rate.** The reference rate for a covered authority is the **lesser** of \u2014\n1. the **median** annual number of invocations in the ten years **preceding the enactment of this Act**; and\n2. the **median** annual number of invocations in the ten years preceding the action.\n\n**The reference rate may fall but may not rise above the rate stated in paragraph (1).**\n\n**(e) Floor for rarely used authorities.** Where the reference rate is less than one invocation per year, **three or more invocations within any twelve-month period is a departure** without regard to subsection (c)(2).\n\n**(f) Invocations not counted.** An invocation that was withdrawn, rescinded, vacated, or held unlawful **is not counted** in computing a reference rate.\n\n**(g) Authorities enacted after this Act.** For a covered authority first enacted after the date of enactment of this Act, the reference rate is the median annual number of invocations in the **first three full years** following enactment, and subsection (c)(2) does not apply during that period. Subsections (c)(1) and (f) apply from the first invocation.\n\n**(h) Where records are incomplete.** Until an agency has published the reconstruction required by \u00a7 1-3(d), **the reference rate is the lowest annual number of invocations for which the agency has records.**\n\n**(i) Automated system.** A computational process that produces an output bearing on whether or how a covered authority is exercised against a person. The term applies **whatever the method by which the output is produced**, whether or not the process is described as artificial intelligence, whether or not it derives its behavior from data, and whether or not a person could produce the same output by other means.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Individualized determination required","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** A covered authority may be exercised against a person **only if**, before the action takes effect, an officer of the agency has made a determination, **as to that person**, and has recorded it in a writing that states:\n1. the statutory provision invoked, **by name**;\n2. the facts particular to that person on which the determination rests;\n3. how those facts satisfy the standard published under \u00a7 1-2; and\n4. the date of the determination and the officer who made it.\n\n**(b) A list is not a determination.** A writing that identifies two or more persons and states a conclusion applicable to all of them, without stating the facts particular to each, does not satisfy subsection (a) as to any of them.\n\n**(c) Contemporaneity.** The writing must be made before the action takes effect. A writing prepared afterward does not satisfy subsection (a), and **no reason not stated in it may be relied upon** to sustain the action.\n\n**(d) The specific ground must be invoked by name.** Where a statute confers authority on a specified ground, a determination reciting a broader or vaguer formulation than the statute uses does not satisfy subsection (a).\n\n**(e) Disclosure.** The writing shall be furnished to the person against whom the action is taken, at or before the time the action takes effect, except that the agency may withhold portions whose disclosure is prohibited by statute, in which case it shall furnish an index of what is withheld and the statutory basis.\n\n**(f) Consequence.** An action taken without a determination satisfying this Section **is of no effect**, and any status, license, clearance, benefit, contract, registration, or authorization it purported to alter continues as if the action had not been taken.\n\n**(f-1) High-volume classes of case: correction before invalidity.** In a class of case in which the agency published, under \u00a7 1-3, more than the number of determinations set in the schedule to this Act in the preceding year, subsection (f) does not operate in the first instance. Instead \u2014\n\n1. a person against whom an action was taken without a determination satisfying this Section may **demand a corrected determination**, in writing, identifying the action;\n2. the agency shall furnish a determination satisfying subsection (a) **within the period set in the schedule to this Act**; and\n3. **if it does not, subsection (f) then operates** as to that action, and the person may proceed under Section 8.\n\n**(f-2) The demand is the only step required.** The process in subsection (f-1) is the sole prerequisite to an action under this Act as to a high-volume class, and **notwithstanding \u00a7 9-1(c)** a person shall make the demand before bringing such an action. **No other administrative remedy, appeal, hearing, or review is a prerequisite**, and no agency may condition the corrected determination on the person forgoing any right.\n\n**(f-3) What subsection (f-1) does and does not change.** Subsection (f-1) changes **when** invalidity attaches in a high-volume class, and nothing else. It does not narrow subsection (a), does not excuse any determination, and does not apply to an **irreversible action**, as to which subsection (f) operates immediately. Where an action takes effect before a corrected determination is furnished, **the action is suspended from the making of a demand under subsection (f-1)(1) until the period in subsection (f-1)(2) has run**, and any status it purported to alter continues during that period."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2a","heading":"\u00a7 2-2A. Reasonable determination time","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) The agency states the time.** Within 90 days of the effective date of this Section, and before first applying a covered authority in a class of case thereafter, each agency shall publish, for each class of case in which it makes determinations, **the time within which it is reasonable to expect a determination**, together with the rationale for that time.\n\n**(b) What a rationale must contain.** The rationale shall identify the basis for the time stated \u2014 the steps the determination requires, the information that must be obtained from another person or agency, the volume of cases in the class, and the agency's own recent performance. **A statement of a time that does not identify a basis does not satisfy subsection (a)**, and neither does one whose only stated basis is the resources available to the agency.\n\n**(b-1) And the time has an outer bound.** A time published under subsection (a) **may not exceed the outer bound set in the schedule to this Act**. A published time exceeding it does not satisfy subsection (a), and subsection (e) operates as though no time had been published.\n\n**(c) Inaction beyond the published time.** Where an agency has not made a determination within the time published under subsection (a), the failure to determine **is an individualized adverse action** for purposes of \u00a7 2-1(a) and of Section 8, and the duty in subsection (d) attaches. **\u00a7 2-2 and \u00a7 2-3 do not apply to it** \u2014 there is no exercise of a covered authority to condition and no invocation to count \u2014 and the remedy is the remedy provided by Section 8 for a failure to perform a duty under subsection (d).\n\n**(d) What the agency must then do.** Within 30 days after the published time has run, the agency shall either make the determination, or furnish the person a writing stating **the facts particular to that person** that have prevented a determination and **the date by which one will be made.** A writing that recites workload, backlog, or resource constraints, without facts particular to that person, does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(e) Where no time is published.** Until an agency publishes under subsection (a) for a class of case, a determination not made within the period set in the schedule to this Act is an individualized adverse action under subsection (c). **Non-publication is not a defense**, and does not suspend subsection (c).\n\n**(f) Tolling.** The time published under subsection (a) is tolled for any period during which the agency has requested information from the person and it has not been furnished, provided the request was in writing and identified what was required. **No other tolling applies.**\n\n**(g) Revision.** An agency may revise a published time **prospectively**, publishing the prior time, the revised time, the date, and the reason. A revision does not apply to a determination already pending, and **a revision made after the published time has run in a particular case has no effect as to that case.**\n\n**(h) Publication of performance.** Each agency shall publish annually, for each class of case, the number of determinations made within the published time, the number made after it, and the number pending beyond it, **the time from request to determination at the median and at the ninetieth percentile**, and **the number of writings furnished under subsection (d)**. The duty to publish under this subsection is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.\n\n**(h-1) A pattern is named for what it is.** Where the counts published under subsection (h) show, for a class of case, determinations made after the published time in more than the share set in the schedule to this Act, **that pattern is evidence that the agency's staffing of that class is inadequate, and shall be stated as such in the publication.**\n\n**(h-2) And the publication says what would fix it.** Where subsection (h-1) applies, the agency shall state in the same publication **the personnel required to make determinations in that class within the published time**, and shall transmit the statement to the Congress and to the Comptroller General. **The statement is not a deadline missed within the meaning of \u00a7 7-3(a)** \u2014 a time published under subsection (a) is the agency's own and not a statutory one \u2014 and the Comptroller General may publish it alongside that compilation without it becoming part of it. The statement is renewed annually until the pattern ends."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2b","heading":"\u00a7 2-2B. A person determines","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) Who determines.** The officer who makes a determination required by \u00a7 2-2, and the officer who states reasons under \u00a7 2-3(a), **shall be a natural person.** The writing required by \u00a7 2-2(a) shall identify that person by name and role, and that person is accountable for the determination. Where the function is performed under an arrangement with the United States, \u00a7 0-7 applies.\n\n**(b) What a system may do.** Nothing in this Section limits the use of an automated system to search, retrieve, organize, transcribe, translate, summarize, calculate, sort, rank, surface, flag, draft, or propose, or to perform any other function in the preparation of a determination. **The use of an automated system is not a violation of this Section**, is not a ground on which any action is invalid, and requires the approval of no officer.\n\n**(c) An output is not a determination.** The output of an automated system is not a determination for purposes of \u00a7 2-2. A writing satisfies \u00a7 2-2(a)(2) and (3) only where the facts particular to the person, and the manner in which those facts satisfy the standard published under \u00a7 1-2, are **stated by the person making the determination.** A writing whose statement of those matters consists of agreement with, adoption of, concurrence in, or reproduction of an automated system's output does not satisfy \u00a7 2-2(a).\n\n**(d) What the writing records.** Where an automated system produced an output recommending, scoring, ranking, or otherwise indicating an outcome as to the person, the writing required by \u00a7 2-2(a) shall state \u2014\n\n1. that an automated system was used, and which system it was;\n2. what its output indicated; and\n3. whether the person making the determination recorded the determination **before** that output was displayed to that person.\n\n**(e) The rate of agreement is published.** Each agency shall publish annually, for each class of case and each component, the number of determinations to which subsection (d) applied, the number in which the determination conformed to the automated system's output, and the number in which the determination was recorded before that output was displayed. **These counts are the only measure of independent determination this Section employs, and they require no one to judge how any particular determination was reached.** The duty to publish under this subsection is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, and a person denied a publication this subsection requires has suffered injury sufficient to support that action.\n\n**(f) A pattern is named for what it is.** Where the counts published under subsection (e) show, for a class of case, conformity with the automated system's output in more than the share set in the schedule to this Act, the agency **shall state in the same publication that determinations in that class are not shown to be independent of that output.** No rate published under this Section makes any determination invalid, and nothing in this subsection confers on any officer authority to approve, disapprove, or review the use of any automated system.\n\n**(g) No inquiry into the sincerity of a review.** This Section is satisfied by the record it describes. Nothing in it requires or permits any officer or court to determine how carefully a person considered a matter, how long the consideration took, or whether the person's agreement with an automated system's output was genuine.\n\n**(h) Consequence.** A determination not made in conformity with this Section does not satisfy \u00a7 2-2(a), and \u00a7 2-2(f) operates accordingly.\n\n**(i) No waiver.** The requirements of this Section may not be waived by agreement, by protocol, or by an agency's internal policy, and are **not satisfied by any certification, attestation, accreditation, audit, impact assessment, or evaluation of an automated system**, whoever conducts it.\n\n**(j) Severability.** Subsections (e) and (f) are severable from subsections (a) through (d). If either is held invalid, subsections (a) through (d) and (g) through (i) continue to operate; and if subsections (a) through (d) are held invalid as applied to any class of covered authority, they continue to operate as to every other."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Departure","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** Where an individualized adverse action departs from ordinary practice, the authority may be exercised **only if** the determination required by \u00a7 2-2 also states the agency's reasons for treating the person differently from those the agency has not subjected to the authority.\n\n**(b) No published standard.** An individualized adverse action is **not effective** unless the agency has published a standard under \u00a7 1-2 covering the class of case, and the determination required by \u00a7 2-2 states the basis on which the action satisfies that standard.\n\n**(c) No published counts.** Where an agency has not published the counts required by \u00a7 1-3 for a covered authority, an individualized adverse action under that authority **is deemed a departure** until the counts are published.\n\n**(d) Production.** In any proceeding in which the validity of the action is at issue, the agency shall produce the determination and the records establishing ordinary practice. **Policy documents are not responsive to a demand for records of practice.**\n\n**(e) No inquiry into the merits.** Nothing in this Section authorizes review of the correctness of a determination, the weight the agency gave any fact, or the wisdom of the action."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Discretion as to outcome is not discretion as to reason","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a)** Where a statute commits an action to the discretion of an agency, that commitment governs **what the agency may decide**. It does not render lawful an action taken **because** of a person's exercise of a right protected by the First Amendment, or because of a characteristic protected by the Constitution or by Federal law.\n\n**(b)** The availability of review of the reason for an action is not affected by the unavailability of review of the action's merits.\n\n**(c)** This Section states a rule of decision for the validity of agency action. It confers no jurisdiction and prescribes no standard of proof."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Personal-determination exceptions","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"Where a statute permits an officer to take an action otherwise prohibited upon the officer's **personal determination**, the determination satisfies the statute only if it is made by that officer in person, is recorded in a writing meeting \u00a7 2-2(a), states the **non-protected basis** for the action, and is furnished to the person under \u00a7 2-2(e). **A determination reciting only the statutory conclusion does not satisfy this Section.**"},{"anchor":"sec-2-6","heading":"\u00a7 2-6. Remedies","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 INDIVIDUALIZED ACTION","body":"**(a) Self-executing.** The consequences stated in \u00a7\u00a7 2-2(f), 2-3(b), and 2-3(c) operate **without any action by any officer or court**.\n\n**(b) Civil action.** A person against whom an action was taken without compliance with \u00a7 2-2 or \u00a7 2-3 may bring a civil action in the district court for a declaration to that effect and for **restoration of the status altered**.\n\n**(c) Fees and interim relief.** The court **shall** award fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff. Interim relief preserving the status quo shall issue on a showing that the determination required by \u00a7 2-2 was not made.\n\n**(d) No new body.** No office, board, or commission is created by this Section, and no consequence provided in it depends on the existence, funding, or staffing of any body."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Covered officer.** An officer or employee of the United States who is authorized to conduct a stop, a search, an arrest, or a detention, or to use force in the course of official duties; and any person performing such a function **under contract with, or at the direction of, the United States.**\n\n**(b) Covered encounter.** An exercise by a covered officer of the authority described in subsection (a) against a person.\n\n**(c) Reportable event.** A covered encounter in which \u2014\n1. a covered officer **discharges a firearm**, other than at a range, in training, or to destroy an injured animal;\n2. force used by a covered officer causes **death or serious bodily injury**; or\n3. a person **dies** in custody, or in the course of or following a covered encounter.\n\n**(d) Serious bodily injury.** Injury involving a substantial risk of death, protracted loss or impairment of a bodily member, organ, or mental faculty, or requiring hospitalization or surgical intervention.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. The investigation of a reportable event is not optional","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Trigger.** A reportable event opens an investigation **by operation of this Section.** No decision, referral, approval, or determination by any officer is a condition of its commencement, and **no officer may decline to open, suspend, or terminate it.**\n\n**(b) Purposes.** The investigation shall determine \u2014\n1. **the facts** of the event;\n2. whether the conduct conformed to law and to the standards the agency has published; and\n3. whether **training, guidance, supervision, or equipment require change** to prevent recurrence.\n\n**The interest of the officer whose conduct is reviewed in being cleared by an impartial inquiry is a purpose of this Section**, and the agency shall state that purpose in the materials by which it implements this Act.\n\n**(c) Independence by structure.** The investigation shall be conducted by a component **outside the chain of command of every officer whose conduct is under review.** A person who was present at the event, or who directed or supervised the operation in which it occurred, **may not participate in or direct the investigation.** An agency may satisfy this subsection by assigning the investigation to another component, to another agency, or to an investigator engaged for the purpose; **this Section does not require the creation of any office, board, or commission.**\n\n**(d) Officers' accounts.** Each covered officer who participated in, or was present at, a reportable event shall provide an account of it **in that officer's own words**, individually attested, **recorded as soon as practicable** and in any event within 72 hours. **An account given by a person who was not present does not satisfy this subsection**, and no account required by it may be given jointly.\n\n**(e) Products preserved and transmitted.** The autopsy, ballistic and forensic products, medical records of the treatment of injury, recordings, the accounts required by subsection (d), and the investigation's findings **shall be preserved and transmitted to the agency's head and to the Attorney General**, whether or not any charge is brought and whether or not any proceeding follows.\n\n**(f) Publication of findings.** The agency shall publish the investigation's **findings of fact** within **180 days** of the event, withholding only such portions as a statute prohibits from disclosure or as would identify a person other than a covered officer without that person's consent, **together with an index of what is withheld and the statutory basis.** Where findings are not published within 180 days, the agency shall publish **a statement of the reason and the expected date**, and shall renew that statement every 90 days until the findings are published.\n\n**(f-1) How long they take, published.** Each agency shall publish annually the number of investigations opened under subsection (a), the number in which findings were published within 180 days, the number published after it, the number pending beyond it, and **the time from event to published findings at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.** The duty is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.\n\n**(f-2) A pattern is named for what it is.** Where the counts published under subsection (f-1) show findings published after 180 days in more than the share set in the schedule to this Act, **that pattern is evidence that the agency's investigative capacity is inadequate, and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with **the personnel required to publish findings within 180 days.** The statement is transmitted to the Congress and renewed annually until the pattern ends.\n\n**(g) Prosecutorial discretion is not displaced.** This Section requires **findings of fact and nothing else.** It requires no conclusion as to whether any charge should be brought, confers no authority to initiate, decline, direct, or review any prosecution, and **limits in no respect the discretion of the Attorney General or of any United States Attorney.** No finding published under this Section binds any court, and none is admissible as a determination of criminal liability.\n\n**(h) Relation to other duties.** Compliance with this Section neither satisfies nor excuses any duty under the Enforceable Rights Act, and the findings published under it are **not a prerequisite** to any claim."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. A withdrawal of investigative capacity is itself reportable","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Duty.** Before guidance takes effect that would **narrow the class of matters an agency or component will investigate** under this Section or under any other authority to investigate the conduct of covered officers, **or that would reduce the personnel assigned to conduct those investigations**, the agency shall publish \u2014\n1. the guidance;\n2. **the class of matter that will no longer be investigated**, stated specifically;\n3. the office or officer whose decision it was;\n4. the date it takes effect; and\n5. where the guidance reduces personnel, **the number of personnel assigned to those investigations before and after it**, and the number assigned on the date of introduction of this Act.\n\n**(b) Ministerial.** The duty imposed by this Section is **ministerial**, and applies whether the guidance is styled as policy, memorandum, priority, resource allocation, or practice.\n\n**(c) No limitation.** This Section confers no power on any person to approve, disapprove, or set aside such guidance. **It requires that the narrowing be published, and nothing else.**\n\n**(e) And the count is published whether or not anyone decided anything.** Each agency employing covered officers shall publish annually **the number of personnel assigned to conduct investigations under \u00a7 3-2**, and that number as it stood on the date of introduction of this Act. The duty is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Recording equipment \u2014 the data, not the encounter","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Annual publication.** Each agency employing covered officers shall publish annually \u2014\n1. the number of recording devices **acquired, deployed, and assigned** to officers performing covered encounters, and the number of such officers;\n2. the number of covered encounters **recorded**, and the number in which a device was assigned but no recording exists;\n3. the **retention period** applied, and the volume of footage retained and destroyed; and\n4. the number of **requests** for footage received, granted, denied, and the grounds of denial.\n\n**(b) Policy.** The agency shall publish the policy governing when recording is required, how footage is retained, and who may access it.\n\n**(c) No equipment mandate.** This Section **requires no agency to acquire or deploy any device.** It requires publication of what the agency has, what it does with it, and what it does not record. Where a device was assigned and no recording exists, the consequence is the one the **Enforceable Rights Act \u00a7 9-4** provides, and this Section adds none."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Warrantless arrests","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Record.** For each arrest made without a warrant, a covered officer shall record, **contemporaneously** \u2014\n1. the date, time, and place;\n2. the **statutory authority** relied upon;\n3. **the facts the officer relied upon** as probable cause, stated specifically;\n4. the officer's individual identifier;\n5. whether force was used, and whether the encounter was recorded; and\n6. whether the person was released without charge, and if so, when.\n\n**(b) Publication of counts.** Each agency shall publish annually the **number of arrests made without a warrant**, disaggregated by component, by the statutory authority relied upon, and by disposition \u2014 charged, released without charge, or transferred. **Individual records are not published under this subsection.**\n\n**(c) One direction only.** A record made under subsection (a) is evidence of what the officer relied upon. **It is not evidence that the facts recited in it were true**, and the absence of a record is not evidence that the arrest was lawful."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Stops not based on individualized suspicion","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Neutral selection required.** An agency conducting stops **not based on individualized suspicion** shall establish and publish, **in advance**, the neutral method by which persons or vehicles are selected \u2014 such as a fixed interval, or a rule defined by location and time \u2014 and the objective the method serves.\n\n**(b) The authority is conditioned.** The authority to conduct a stop not based on individualized suspicion **is conditioned on compliance with subsection (a).** An agency that has not published a method for a class of stop **may not conduct that class of stop.**\n\n**(b-1) What this Section reaches.** A stop under this Section is a detention of a person or vehicle, however brief, in which the person is not free to leave. **It does not reach screening conducted as a condition of entry to a facility, a conveyance, or a secured area, where the screening is applied to every person seeking entry, is announced in advance, and is no more extensive than entry requires.** Screening that is applied selectively among persons seeking entry is a stop under this Section, and subsection (a) governs the method of selection. This subsection states the boundary; it does not narrow subsection (a) as to any detention.\n\n**(c) Group characteristics are not suspicion.** Race, ethnicity, national origin, religion, language, accent, or apparent immigration status **shall not be used** as a criterion of selection under subsection (a), nor as a factor contributing to individualized suspicion, **except** as an element of a specific description of a particular suspect sought.\n\n**(d) Counts.** Each agency shall publish annually, for each published method, the **number of stops conducted** under it, the number resulting in a search, and the number resulting in an arrest or citation."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Enforcement of this Section","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"**(a) Ministerial and enforceable.** The duties imposed by \u00a7\u00a7 3-2(f), 3-3, 3-4, 3-5(b), and 3-6(a) and (d) are **ministerial** and enforceable as provided in **\u00a7 1-4**, in an action in the nature of mandamus by **any person**, without regard to whether that person was subjected to a covered encounter.\n\n**(b) No new body.** No office, board, or commission is created by this Section, and **no duty or consequence provided in it depends on the existence, funding, or staffing of any body.**\n\n**(c) Anti-substitution.** Where an agency ceases to exercise a function to which this Section applies and the function is performed instead by another agency, by a State, local, or Tribal agency under agreement, or by a person under contract, **the duties of this Section apply according to their terms** to the entity performing it."},{"anchor":"sec-3-8","heading":"\u00a7 3-8. Severability","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 RECORDS OF COERCIVE ENCOUNTERS","body":"This Section is severable from every other provision of this Act, and each subsection of this Section is severable from every other. **The application of this Section to any class of encounter is severable from its application to every other class.** If any provision of this Section, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Section and its application to other persons and circumstances shall not be affected, and the remainder of this Act shall continue in effect. Congress would have enacted this Section independently of any provision or application held invalid."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"In this Section \u2014\n\n**(a) Covered order.** A direction to a covered official to take, or to refrain from taking, an action that \u2014\n\n**(1)** is an individualized adverse action within the meaning of \u00a7 2-1(a);\n\n**(2)** involves the use of physical force against a person, the detention or restraint of a person, or entry into a dwelling; or\n\n**(3)** is a direction not to perform a covered duty within the meaning of \u00a7 0-4(a)(2), or to delay its performance beyond the time performance is due.\n\n**(b) However denominated.** A direction is a covered order without regard to the term used to describe it, including order, direction, instruction, directive, guidance, request, expectation, or priority, and without regard to whether compliance is stated to be optional.\n\n**(c) What is not a covered order.** A direction is a covered order **only if it identifies the action to be taken and the matter or the person to which it applies.** The following are not covered orders: a statement of general policy; an enforcement priority; a guidance document of general applicability; an allocation of personnel or resources; and an instruction as to the order in which matters are to be taken up. **Any of them is a covered order to the extent it directs a specific action in a specified matter or against a specified person.**\n\n**(d) Issuing official.** The covered official who gives a covered order, or who transmits, relays, or adopts a covered order given by another. **Where a covered order originates with the President, the issuing official is the covered official who transmits, relays, or adopts it.**\n\n**(e) Directed official.** The covered official to whom a covered order is given.\n\n**(f) Covered official.** Has the meaning given in \u00a7 0-4(a)(1).\n\n**(g) Exclusions.** No direction is a covered order to the extent it is given \u2014\n\n**(1)** in the exercise of the President's authority as Commander in Chief;\n\n**(2)** within the chain of command of the armed forces; or\n\n**(3)** to a person subject to chapter 47 of title 10, United States Code, in that capacity.\n\nNothing in this Section applies to the exercise of the pardon power, the conduct of diplomatic relations, or the nomination of officers.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. A covered order is recorded on request before refusal may be penalized","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** Where a directed official has made a request under subsection (d), an agency may take an adverse action against that official for declining to carry out the covered order **only if** the writing described in subsection (b) was furnished within the time allowed by subsection (c).\n\n**(b) Contents.** The writing shall state \u2014\n\n**(1)** the action directed, in terms specific enough to identify what the directed official was to do;\n\n**(2)** the person or persons, if any, against whom the action was to be taken;\n\n**(3)** the statutory or other legal authority relied upon, **by name and by provision**;\n\n**(4)** the date and time the order was given; and\n\n**(5)** the name and position of the issuing official, **who shall sign it**.\n\n**(c) Time; exigency.** The issuing official shall furnish the writing not later than `[SCHEDULE: hours]` after the request. Where the action must be carried out before a writing can be furnished, the issuing official shall **state orally the matters described in subsection (b)(1) through (3)**, and shall furnish the writing not later than `[SCHEDULE: hours]` after the action is carried out. An oral statement so made satisfies subsection (a) for the period allowed by this subsection, and does not satisfy it thereafter.\n\n**(d) The request, and the absence of one.** A directed official may request the writing at any time before the action is to be carried out. **The request shall be made in a form that creates a record, which may be electronic, and need not state a reason.**\n\n**Where no request is made, this Section requires no writing.** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of an agency to give a covered order orally, to act upon one, or to take an adverse action against an official who declines one, where no request under this subsection was made.\n\n**(e) Classified and statutorily protected material.** Where the writing cannot be furnished in full to the directed official without disclosing properly classified information, or information whose disclosure another statute prohibits, the issuing official shall furnish it at the highest level for which the directed official is cleared and shall in every case furnish, in a form the directed official may retain, **the matters described in subsection (b)(1), (b)(3) and (b)(5)**. Nothing in this subsection authorizes review of a classification decision or of a determination that information is protected from disclosure by another statute.\n\n**(f) Anti-substitution.** Where a covered order has not been recorded as this Section requires, and **substantially the same action against substantially the same person** is then sought through another instrument, request, or channel, **the conditions of this Section apply to that instrument, request, or channel according to their terms.**\n\n**(g) Proof is documentary on both sides.** In any proceeding under this Section, the request and the writing are established by the records of them and not otherwise.\n\n**(1)** Where the directed official produces no record of a request, **subsection (a) does not apply.**\n\n**(2)** Where the agency produces the writing, or a contemporaneous record of the oral statement described in subsection (c), **subsection (a) is satisfied.**\n\n**(3)** Where the official produces a record of a request and the agency produces neither, subsection (a) is not satisfied, and **no other evidence of the order's contents may supply it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. An opinion offered as protection is furnished","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) Condition on the representation.** No covered official may represent to a directed official that a legal opinion, memorandum, or written advice supports the lawfulness of a covered order **unless the opinion, memorandum, or advice is furnished to that official** in a form the official may retain.\n\n**(b) The condition runs to the representation, not to the document.** Nothing in this Section requires that any opinion, memorandum, or advice be produced, published, disclosed to any other person, or created. **An agency that does not invoke an opinion as support for a covered order owes no duty under this Section.**\n\n**(c) Privilege.** Furnishing a document under this Section is a disclosure within the executive branch to an official directed to act upon it, and **is not a waiver of any privilege as to any other person or proceeding.** Where the document cannot be furnished in full for a reason stated in \u00a7 4-2(e), the covered official may not make the representation.\n\n**(d) No inference.** The furnishing of a document under this Section is not evidence that the covered order was lawful, and the absence of a document is not evidence that it was unlawful."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Protection of the official who declines","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** No adverse action may be taken against a directed official because the official \u2014\n\n**(1)** declined to carry out a covered order in circumstances in which **\u00a7 4-2(a) bars an adverse action for that refusal**;\n\n**(2)** requested the writing described in \u00a7 4-2(b), or requested a document under \u00a7 4-3; or\n\n**(3)** stated to the issuing official or to any other covered official that the official understood the covered order to be unlawful, **whether or not that understanding was correct.**\n\n**(b) Employees who are not officers.** As to a directed official who is not an officer of the United States within the meaning of the Appointments Clause, an adverse action taken in violation of subsection (a) is **void**, and the official's pay, status, and duties continue by operation of law.\n\n**(c) Officers.** As to a directed official who is an officer of the United States, an adverse action taken within `[SCHEDULE: days]` after conduct described in subsection (a) is effective **only upon** a written statement of the grounds for the action, signed by the officer taking it, published, and transmitted to the Committees on the Judiciary of the Senate and the House of Representatives.\n\n**(d) Civil action.** A directed official against whom an adverse action was taken in violation of subsection (a) may bring a civil action in the district courts of the United States for a declaration to that effect, for restoration of pay, status, and duties, and for costs and reasonable attorney's fees. **The court shall award fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff.**\n\n**(e) No tenure conferred.** Nothing in this Section confers tenure in office, limits removal upon grounds unrelated to the conduct described in subsection (a), or requires that any officer be reinstated.\n\n**(f) The compliant official.** The fact that a directed official carried out a covered order for which the writing required by \u00a7 4-2 was furnished **is not, of itself, a ground for an adverse action against that official.** Nothing in this subsection limits any other ground for an adverse action, and nothing in it affects any liability arising under any other law."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Where the duty attaches","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) The issuing official.** The duty to make and furnish the writing required by \u00a7 4-2, and the condition stated in \u00a7 4-3(a), run to **the issuing official**. They do not run to the directed official.\n\n**(b) No consequence at the point of execution.** No consequence provided by this Section attaches to a directed official by reason of the issuing official's failure to make, furnish, or record anything this Section requires.\n\n**(c) Failure to record *on request* is reportable.** Where a directed official has made a request under \u00a7 4-2(d), the writing was not furnished within the time allowed by \u00a7 4-2(c), and the covered order was nonetheless carried out, the agency shall publish, not later than `[SCHEDULE: days]` thereafter, **the fact that a requested record was not made, the authority under which the action was taken, and the position of the issuing official.** The publication **shall not identify the person against whom the action was taken, nor the directed official who made the request**, and \u00a7 4-2(e) applies to it.\n\n**(c-1) No general publication duty.** Subsection (c) is triggered by an unanswered request and by nothing else. **A covered order carried out without a writing, where no request was made, requires no publication under this Section.**\n\n**(d) Nothing about lawfulness.** A publication under subsection (c) states that a record is absent. **It states no conclusion as to the lawfulness of the order or of the action taken.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. A statement of the law is not misconduct","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) Adverse action prohibited.** No adverse action may be taken against a covered official because the official stated \u2014 publicly or to another official \u2014 that officers and employees of the United States are obliged not to carry out unlawful orders, or that their obligation runs to the Constitution and the laws of the United States.\n\n**(b) Not evidence of misconduct.** A statement described in subsection (a) is **not evidence of misconduct, of unfitness, or of a want of candor, reliability, or loyalty**, in any proceeding concerning the official's employment, security clearance, or fitness for office.\n\n**(c) Scope.** Subsections (a) and (b) apply to a statement that **does not identify a particular order** and **does not direct or counsel any identified person to disobey a particular order.** They confer no protection upon conduct beyond the statement itself.\n\n**(d) Investigative and prosecutorial authority unaffected.** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of any officer to open, conduct, or decline any investigation, to seek or decline to seek any charge, or to exercise any prosecutorial discretion, and nothing in it confers authority to review any such decision. **This Section governs the grounds upon which an adverse action affecting employment may be taken, and the use that may be made of a statement in a proceeding concerning employment, and nothing else.**\n\n**(e) Remedy.** Subsections (a) and (b) are enforceable as provided in \u00a7 4-4(b), (c) and (d)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-7","heading":"\u00a7 4-7. Enforcement","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) Self-executing.** The consequences stated in \u00a7\u00a7 4-2(a), 4-4(b) and 4-6(a) operate **without any action by any officer or court.**\n\n**(b) Ministerial.** The publication duty imposed by \u00a7 4-5(c) is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, as provided in \u00a7 1-4.\n\n**(c) No new body.** No office, board, or commission is created by this Section, and no duty or consequence provided in it depends on the existence, funding, or staffing of any body."},{"anchor":"sec-4-8","heading":"\u00a7 4-8. Construction","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"**(a) The question under this Section.** The question in any proceeding under this Section is **whether the writing required by \u00a7 4-2 was made and furnished**, and whether an adverse action was taken for a reason subsection (a) of \u00a7 4-4 or \u00a7 4-6 prohibits. **It is not whether the covered order was lawful.** No provision of this Section authorizes any tribunal to determine the lawfulness of a covered order, and none requires that determination to be made.\n\n**(b) No privilege of disobedience.** This Section **imposes no duty upon any official to decline any order** and creates no privilege of disobedience; a refusal is not made lawful by this Section. **The prohibitions stated in \u00a7\u00a7 4-4 and 4-6 apply according to their terms without regard to whether a refusal was otherwise authorized.** This Section provides only that certain adverse actions may not be taken.\n\n**(c) The President.** This Section imposes no duty upon the President, subjects no action of the President to any requirement or consequence established by this Section, and confers no authority upon any tribunal with respect to any action of the President. **Where a covered order originates with the President, the duties of this Section run to the covered official who transmits, relays, or adopts it**, and to no one else.\n\n**(d) Criminal defenses unaffected.** This Section **creates no defense in any criminal prosecution and abolishes none.** Compliance with this Section is not a defense; non-compliance is not an element of any offense. Nothing in this Section addresses the availability, scope, or effect of any defense founded upon obedience to orders.\n\n**(e) No injunction against issuance.** Nothing in this Section authorizes any tribunal to enjoin, delay, or condition the issuance of any order, or to require that any order be submitted to any person before it is given.\n\n**(f) Direction of subordinates.** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of the President or of any covered official to direct a subordinate, to supervise the performance of a subordinate's duties, or to remove a subordinate on grounds unrelated to conduct described in \u00a7 4-4(a) or \u00a7 4-6(a). **This Section governs the grounds upon which an adverse action may be taken; it does not govern what may be ordered.**\n\n**(g) Exclusions.** The exclusions from this Section are stated at **\u00a7 4-1(g)**, which applies according to its terms. The military half of the conduct this Section addresses is governed by the Emergency and War Powers Act, and nothing in this Section applies to it.\n\n**(h) Existing protections preserved.** Nothing in this Section limits any right, remedy, or protection available under chapter 12 or chapter 23 of title 5, United States Code, or under any other law. **No procedure established by another law is a prerequisite to an action under this Section**, and \u00a7 9-1(c) applies.\n\n**(i) Relation to \u00a7 0-4.** A constitutional assertion within the meaning of \u00a7 0-4(a)(3) does not satisfy \u00a7 4-2(b)(3), and does not excuse the making or furnishing of any writing this Section requires.\n\n**(j) Application.** This Section applies to a covered order given, and to a statement described in \u00a7 4-6(a) made, on or after the effective date of this Act. Nothing in this Section declares any past conduct unlawful or attaches any consequence to conduct occurring before that date."},{"anchor":"sec-4-9","heading":"\u00a7 4-9. Severability","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 THE REFUSAL FUNCTION","body":"This Section is severable from every other provision of this Act, and each subsection of this Section is severable from every other. **\u00a7 4-6 is severable from \u00a7\u00a7 4-2 through 4-5**, the protection of a statement of law being independent of any order or record. If any provision of this Section, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Section and its application to other persons and circumstances shall not be affected, and the remainder of this Act shall continue in effect."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) Legal interpretation.** A written statement of the government's understanding of what a statute, regulation, or constitutional provision requires, permits, or forbids, prepared by or for an agency or the Executive Office of the President, on which an agency relies in exercising authority, or which the government invokes for a purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a). The term includes an opinion of the Office of Legal Counsel, an agency general counsel's opinion, a legal memorandum, a guidance document stating a legal conclusion, and a directive stating the legal basis for an action.\n\n**(b) Adverse action.** An exercise of authority that denies, suspends, revokes, or conditions a benefit, status, license, or authorization; imposes a penalty, charge, or restraint; authorizes a stop, search, seizure, arrest, detention, entry, or use of force; or otherwise operates to the legal detriment of an identified person.\n\n**(c) Published.** Made available to the public, in a form that may be **retained, searched, and cited**, at a location the agency maintains for the purpose and identifies, without charge and without a request. **A document that may be read but not retained is not published**, and neither is a document furnished only to persons the agency selects.\n\n**(d) Furnished.** Delivered in a retainable form to a person, together with the date it was made.\n\n**(e) Irreversible action.** An adverse action whose effect cannot be substantially undone by a later order, including the use of deadly force, a removal from the United States, a transfer to the custody of another sovereign, and the destruction or permanent alienation of property.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Publication as a condition of reliance","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) The condition.** A legal interpretation that was not published, or furnished to the person against whom the action was taken, before the action was taken is not available to the United States, to an agency, or to any officer or employee \u2014\n\n1. as a defense to any claim;\n2. as evidence of good faith, reasonable belief, or the absence of knowledge or willfulness;\n3. as a ground for any defense of reliance on advice of counsel, or for any immunity or privilege whose availability depends on the reasonableness of the officer's belief about the law; or\n4. as a basis for deference to, or the reasonableness of, the agency's construction of any provision of law.\n\n**(a-1) Furnishing cures only the case it reaches.** Where a legal interpretation was furnished to the person against whom the action was taken but was not published, it is available under subsection (a) **only in a proceeding brought by or on behalf of that person, and as to that action.** It remains unavailable for every purpose described in subsection (a) as against any other person. **Furnishing an interpretation to the one person who learns of it does not make it available generally**, and no such furnishing is publication for purposes of this Section.\n\n**(b) Irreversible action.** Where the action is an irreversible action, a legal interpretation is available for a purpose described in subsection (a) only if it was **published** before the action was taken. Furnishing to the person alone does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(c) The condition runs to the document, not to the decision.** This Section does not make an action unlawful, does not create a cause of action, and does not bear on whether an interpretation is correct. It determines only what the government may invoke, and a court applying this Section decides only **whether a writing was published or furnished, and when** \u2014 never whether the writing was right.\n\n**(d) Production is a condition of invocation, not a duty to disclose.** Where the United States, an agency, or an officer or employee invokes a legal interpretation for a purpose described in subsection (a), the interpretation shall be produced to the court and, subject to any protective order and to any procedure otherwise provided by law for classified information, to every other party. **The government may decline to produce it; the consequence of declining is that the interpretation is not invoked and is not before the court.** No characterization of its contents by counsel substitutes for the document, and nothing in this subsection compels the disclosure of any document the government elects not to invoke.\n\n**(e) No inference from silence.** The absence of a published or furnished interpretation is not evidence that an action was unlawful, and creates no presumption against the government on any question other than those stated in subsections (a) and (b)."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Classified and privileged interpretations","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) Withholding is permitted; reliance is what is conditioned.** Nothing in this Section requires the disclosure of any document. An agency may withhold a legal interpretation in whole or in part on any ground available to it, including classification, the attorney-client privilege, the deliberative-process privilege, and the presidential-communications privilege. **The consequence of withholding is the consequence stated in \u00a7 5-2 and no other**, and no adverse inference, sanction, or penalty attaches to a decision to withhold.\n\n**(b) The classified alternative.** Where a legal interpretation is properly classified, \u00a7 5-2(a) and (b) are satisfied as to that interpretation if, before the action, it was transmitted in full to \u2014\n\n1. the Committees on the Judiciary of the House of Representatives and of the Senate; and\n2. each committee of either House with jurisdiction over the agency or the authority relied upon;\n\ntogether with an unclassified summary stating the statutory provision construed and the conclusion reached, published under \u00a7 5-1(c). **The summary states the conclusion; it is not required to state the reasoning.**\n\n**(c) Lethal force.** Where the action authorized is the use of deadly force, transmission under subsection (b) shall also be made to the Committees on Armed Services and on Intelligence of each House. **A legal interpretation authorizing the use of deadly force that was not published under \u00a7 5-2(b) or transmitted under this subsection is not available for any purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a).**\n\n**(b-1) Access for counsel where the interpretation is invoked.** Where an interpretation transmitted under subsection (b) is invoked in a proceeding for a purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a), the court shall, on the application of a party, make it available to counsel holding an appropriate clearance, on the terms and under the procedures applicable to classified information in that proceeding. **The government may decline; the consequence of declining is the consequence stated in \u00a7 5-2 and no other.** This subsection compels no declassification and alters no classification decision.\n\n**(d) A withheld portion.** Where an interpretation is published in part, \u00a7 5-2 is satisfied only as to the published part, and the withheld part is not available for any purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Changed interpretations","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) When this Section applies.** This Section applies where an agency applies to a person a legal interpretation that departs from an interpretation the agency previously maintained, and the previous interpretation appears in \u2014\n\n1. a standard published under \u00a7 1-2;\n2. a regulation, published opinion, published guidance document, or filed litigating position of the agency; or\n3. a written interpretation the agency furnished to any person.\n\n**(a-1) The baseline is bounded and documentary.** An interpretation is one the agency previously maintained, for purposes of subsection (a), only if it appears in a document described in that subsection and was not superseded by a later such document for a continuous period of not less than the period set in the schedule to this Act. **Whether that is so is determined from those documents and their dates**, and not from any characterization by the agency. A refinement, clarification, or application of an interpretation to new facts is not a departure.\n\n**(b) Publication before reliance.** A departing interpretation is not available for any purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a) unless the agency has published, before the action \u2014\n\n1. the departing interpretation;\n2. the previous interpretation and the documents in which it appeared; and\n3. a statement of the legal basis for the departure.\n\n**(c) Prospective only.** A departing interpretation applies only to conduct occurring, or actions initiated, after the publication required by subsection (b). This subsection does not disturb \u00a7 1-2(c), which governs amendment of a published standard.\n\n**(d) The statement of basis is not reviewed for adequacy.** A court applying this Section determines whether the statement required by subsection (b)(3) was published and whether it identifies the previous interpretation and the departure. **It does not determine whether the departure is justified, and this Section confers no authority on any court to prescribe an interpretation.**\n\n**(e) Rulemaking unaffected.** Nothing in this Section requires notice-and-comment rulemaking, converts a legal interpretation into a rule, or bears on whether any document is subject to any requirement of chapter 5 of title 5, United States Code."},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. What publication requires","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) The register.** Each agency shall maintain a public register of the legal interpretations it has published under this Section, identifying for each the date it was made, the date it was published, the statutory provision construed, and whether it remains in effect.\n\n**(b) Withdrawal is published.** Where an agency withdraws or supersedes a published legal interpretation, it shall publish that fact and the date. **A withdrawn interpretation remains available under \u00a7 5-2 as to an action taken while it was in effect.**\n\n**(c) Existence is not classified by default.** Where an interpretation is withheld under \u00a7 5-3 **and has been relied upon in taking an adverse action**, the agency shall publish the fact that an interpretation exists, the statutory provision construed, and the date it was made, **unless a determination is made in writing that publishing those facts would itself disclose properly classified information.** The determination is made by the agency's senior official responsible for classification, or, **where the interpretation authorizes an irreversible action, by the head of the agency personally.** A determination under this subsection shall be recorded and shall state the ground; it is not required to be published.\n\n**(c-1) Irreversible action: existence published in advance.** Where an agency takes an irreversible action and an unpublished legal interpretation authorizes or is relied upon in taking it, the agency shall publish, **before the action**, the fact that an interpretation exists, the statutory provision construed, and the date it was made. This subsection applies **whether or not the agency invokes the interpretation for any purpose described in \u00a7 5-2(a)**, and the determination permitted by subsection (c) applies to it. **\u00a7 5-2(b) governs what may be relied upon; this subsection governs what the public is told exists.**\n\n**(d) The count.** Each agency shall publish annually, as to legal interpretations relied upon in taking an adverse action during the year, the number published, the number withheld in whole, the number withheld in part, and the number transmitted under \u00a7 5-3(b), **without identifying any withheld interpretation.** No agency is required to count or report an interpretation not so relied upon."},{"anchor":"sec-5-6","heading":"\u00a7 5-6. Construction","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 SECRET LAW","body":"**(a) No compelled disclosure.** This Section compels the disclosure of no document, abrogates no privilege, and creates no exception to any statute governing classified information. **Every consequence it imposes falls on what the government may invoke, not on what it must reveal.**\n\n**(b) The advice function is untouched.** Nothing in this Section limits who may request legal advice, who may give it, what it may say, when it may be given, or the confidentiality of the request. **The Attorney General's authority to advise the President and the heads of departments is unaffected in every respect**, and no provision of this Section confers authority on any person to review, approve, or prescribe the content of legal advice.\n\n**(c) No charging authority.** Nothing in this Section confers authority to initiate or decline a prosecution, bears upon any charging decision, or requires or forbids any exercise of prosecutorial discretion.\n\n**(d) Article II.** Nothing in this Section limits the President's authority as Commander in Chief, the conduct of foreign relations, or any function the Constitution commits exclusively to the President, and **no provision of this Section conditions the President's authority to act or requires any interpretation to be published.** This Section operates only as a rule governing what may be invoked in a proceeding \u2014 a subject within Congress's authority to prescribe rules of evidence, procedure, and defense \u2014 and where an interpretation concerns a function committed exclusively to the President, transmission under \u00a7 5-3(b) satisfies \u00a7 5-2 in full.\n\n**(e) Relationship to \u00a7 1-2.** \u00a7 1-2 requires an agency to publish the legal standard it applies to a class of case. This Section governs the use that may be made of a legal interpretation and imposes no duty to publish one. **A published standard under \u00a7 1-2 is a published legal interpretation for purposes of this Section**, and an agency satisfying \u00a7 1-2 as to a class of case satisfies \u00a7 5-2 as to that standard.\n\n**(e-1) Application in time.** \u00a7 5-2 and \u00a7 5-4 apply to an action taken on or after the date 180 days after enactment. **A legal interpretation made before enactment is available under \u00a7 5-2 as to an action taken before that date**, and, as to an action taken on or after it, may be published or transmitted under \u00a7 5-3(b) at any time before the action. No agency is required to publish, review, or inventory an interpretation made before enactment.\n\n**(g) Relationship to section 552 of title 5.** This Section does not reduce, repeal, or supersede any requirement or consequence of section 552 of title 5, United States Code. Where that section is stricter, it continues to apply; where this Section is stricter, it governs. **Actual and timely notice within the meaning of section 552(a)(1) or (a)(2) does not satisfy this Section**, and indexing or availability under section 552(a)(2) satisfies this Section only where it also satisfies \u00a7 5-1(c). The omission from this Section of any limitation appearing in section 552 \u2014 including the limitation of section 552(a)(2) to reliance **by an agency**, and to reliance **as precedent** \u2014 **is a determination by Congress that this Section is not so limited**, and not a gap to be filled by reference to that section.\n\n**(f) Severability.** Each subsection of this Section is severable. If \u00a7 5-4 is held invalid, \u00a7 5-2 and \u00a7 5-3 remain in force. If the application of this Section to interpretations concerning a function committed exclusively to the President is held invalid, **the Section remains in force as to every other interpretation**, and the invalidity does not affect its application to any agency other than the Executive Office of the President."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Covered record.** A record, as defined in section 3301 of title 44, United States Code, that \u2014\n\n1. this Act requires to be made, published, or furnished;\n2. documents a covered encounter under Section 3, an order or refusal under Section 4, or a legal interpretation under Section 5;\n3. is the subject of a pending request under section 552 of title 5, a pending action in any court, a pending inquiry by an Inspector General, or a pending request by a committee or subcommittee of either House acting under the rules of that House; or\n4. is described in a preservation demand under \u00a7 6-4.\n\n**(b) Retention period.** The period set in the schedule to this Act, running from the making of the record, or until the matter to which the record relates is closed, whichever is later, **and in no event longer than the outer period set in the schedule.**\n\n**(b-1) When a matter is closed.** A matter is closed when the request, action, inquiry, or demand that made the record a covered record has been withdrawn, denied with no review pending, or finally resolved with no period for review running. **An agency shall publish the date on which it treats a matter as closed**, and the retention period runs from that published date.\n\n**(c) Ephemeral messaging.** An electronic messaging application, account, setting, or configuration that deletes a message automatically after a period, on reading, or on a command that does not itself leave a record of what was deleted.\n\n**(d) Destruction.** Deletion, erasure, overwriting, defacement, alteration, transfer out of the custody of the United States, or removal from public access. **Configuring a system to delete a record is destruction of that record**, whether or not any person acts at the time of deletion.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. The retention duty","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Duty.** A covered record shall be retained, unaltered and in a form permitting reproduction, for the retention period. **A covered record shall not be destroyed during that period.**\n\n**(b) Existing schedules do not authorize destruction of a covered record.** A records schedule, general records schedule, disposition authority, or retention policy does not authorize the destruction of a covered record during the retention period, and no such schedule or authority is a defense under this Section.\n\n**(c) The absence of the record is the wrong.** Where a covered record was not retained as this Section requires, **the failure to retain it is a violation of this Act**, without regard to whether any person intended the destruction, and without regard to whether the record would have been favourable to any party. This subsection states the wrong; \u00a7 6-6 states the consequence.\n\n**(d) Publication of destruction.** An agency that has destroyed a covered record shall publish, within 15 days of learning of the destruction, the class of record destroyed, the date and manner of destruction, the retention authority relied upon if any, and whether the record has been recovered. **A statement that records \"may have been\" destroyed, without identifying the class, does not satisfy this subsection.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Ephemeral and non-official messaging","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Ephemeral messaging may not be used for a covered record.** No officer or employee shall create or transmit a covered record using ephemeral messaging, and no agency shall configure, adopt, or permit ephemeral messaging for a class of communication in which covered records are made.\n\n**(b) The duty is on the agency, not on the sender.** Compliance with subsection (a) is discharged by the agency's configuration of the systems it provides. **An officer or employee who uses a system the agency has provided and configured does not violate this Section**, and the duty to configure is not delegable to the user.\n\n**(c) Non-official accounts and personal devices.** Where a covered record is created or transmitted on a non-official account or a personal device, a complete copy shall be transmitted to an official account **on the same day**, and the record is a covered record from the moment it is made. **The twenty-day period allowed by section 2209(a)(2) of title 44 is not a safe harbour for a covered record under this Act**, and compliance with that period does not satisfy this subsection. This sentence states a standard for agency records and takes no position on the operation of section 2209 as to the records it governs.\n\n**(d) Configuration is published, not assumed.** Each agency shall publish annually the messaging systems it provides, the retention setting applied to each, and each class of communication for which ephemeral messaging is permitted. **A system not published is not a system the agency has configured for purposes of subsection (b)**, and the duty falls on the agency in any event."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Preservation by operation of law","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Ex parte applications.** Where an agency makes an ex parte application to a court, the record supporting the application \u2014 including every representation made and every document furnished \u2014 **is preserved by operation of law until the matter is closed and any period for review has run.** No demand, order, or notice is required for this subsection to operate.\n\n**(b) Preservation demand.** Any person may serve on an agency a written demand identifying, with reasonable particularity, records the person seeks to have preserved. **Records so identified are covered records from the time the demand is received.**\n\n**(c) A demand lapses unless someone acts on it.** A demand under subsection (b) ceases to have effect at the end of the period set in the schedule to this Act unless, within that period, the person has made a request under section 552 of title 5 or commenced an action to which the records are material, or a court has extended it. A demand that has lapsed may be renewed only on a statement of what has changed.\n\n**(d) The demand does not compel production.** A demand under subsection (b) requires preservation only. It confers no right of access, creates no obligation to search, produce, review, index, or log, and is not a request under section 552 of title 5.\n\n**(e) A demand that is not reasonably particular.** An agency may publish a statement that a demand does not identify records with reasonable particularity, specifying which parts are deficient. **The records identified remain covered for 30 days after that publication**, within which the person may cure the demand or seek an order; absent a cure or an order the demand lapses as to the deficient parts and continues as to the rest."},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Records already public","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Withdrawal from public access.** A record that an agency has made available to the public shall not be removed from public access during the retention period. Where a record is removed, the agency shall publish the fact, the date, the identity of the official who directed it, and the ground.\n\n**(b) Correction is not withdrawal.** This Section does not prevent the correction of a record, the addition of a superseding version, or the redaction of information whose disclosure is prohibited by law, **provided the fact and scope of the change are published and the prior version remains available or its unavailability is published.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-6","heading":"\u00a7 6-6. Consequence","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) The record may not be relied upon in its absence.** Where a covered record was not retained as this Section requires, the United States, the agency, and any officer or employee **may not rely on the absence of that record**, and may not assert that a fact the record would have established is unproven, in any proceeding.\n\n**(b) Adverse inference.** In any action in which the content of a destroyed covered record is material, the court **shall** presume that the record would not have supported the position of the party that failed to retain it. **The presumption is rebuttable only by producing the record or an accurate copy of it.**\n\n**(c) Ministerial duties are enforceable.** The duties to publish under \u00a7 6-2(d), \u00a7 6-3(d) and \u00a7 6-5(a) are ministerial and enforceable in an action for mandamus **by any person who has sought the publication and not received it.** The injury redressed is the denial of information to which this Act entitles the person \u2014 the concrete informational injury recognized in *FEC v. Akins*, 524 U.S. 11 (1998) \u2014 and no further showing is required. **This subsection is severable**; if it is held not to present a case or controversy, every other provision of this Section, including the consequences under subsections (a) and (b), remains in force and operates without it.\n\n**(d) No consequence turns on a determination by any officer.** Nothing in this Section requires, permits, or depends upon a determination by the Attorney General, the Archivist, a supervisor, or any other officer, and **no consequence provided by this Section is withheld by reason of any officer's decision not to act.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-7","heading":"\u00a7 6-7. Relationship to existing law","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Chapters 21, 22, 29, 31 and 33 of title 44 are not displaced.** This Section adds to the obligations imposed by those chapters and reduces none of them. Where they are stricter, they continue to apply; where this Section is stricter, it governs.\n\n**(b) The limitations of section 3106 do not carry into this Section.** Section 3106 of title 44 provides for recovery of removed records through action initiated by the Attorney General, and permits the Archivist only to **request** that such action be initiated. **The omission from this Section of any requirement that an officer initiate, request, approve, or concur in anything is a determination by Congress that no such requirement applies**, and is not a gap to be filled by reference to that section. A failure to notify or to request under section 3106 neither satisfies nor excuses any duty under this Section.\n\n**(c) The limitations of section 2209 do not carry into this Section.** Section 2209 of title 44 reaches Presidential and Vice Presidential records created on non-official accounts, allows twenty days for forwarding, and makes the consequence a disciplinary determination by a supervisor. **This Section reaches agency records, reaches official accounts, allows no such period as to ephemeral messaging, and provides consequences that depend on no supervisor's determination.** Each of those differences is a determination by Congress.\n\n**(d) Criminal provisions untouched.** Nothing in this Section limits, displaces, or is displaced by section 1519 or section 2071 of title 18, United States Code. **This Section requires no intent and creates no criminal liability**, and a failure to retain under this Section is not, of itself, evidence of any offense.\n\n**(e) Presidential records.** This Section applies to a record of an agency. It does not apply to a Presidential record or Vice Presidential record within the meaning of chapter 22 of title 44, and **states no position on the application of that chapter.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-8","heading":"\u00a7 6-8. Construction and severability","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PRESERVATION OF THE RECORD","body":"**(a) No records-creation duty.** This Section requires the retention of records that exist or that another provision of law requires to be made. **It requires no record to be created**, and imposes no obligation to search, review, index, or produce.\n\n**(b) Volume.** The duty under \u00a7 6-2 attaches to a covered record as defined in \u00a7 6-1(a) and to no other record. **The great majority of federal records are outside this Section**, and nothing in it authorizes any officer to extend it by designation.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each Section, subsection and paragraph is severable. **If \u00a7 6-4(b) is held invalid, the preservation by operation of law under \u00a7 6-4(a) is unaffected**; if \u00a7 6-6(b) is held invalid, \u00a7 6-6(a) and (c) remain in force; and if this Section is held invalid as applied to any class of record, it remains in force as to every other class."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Every deadline carries a default","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 STATUTORY DUTIES AND DEADLINES","body":"**(a)** Where a provision of Federal law requires an officer or agency to submit a report, publish a document, install or deliver an object, or take any other **ministerial** action by a date certain, and the action has not been taken by that date:\n\n**(1)** the duty is **ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person**, and a person denied information the provision requires to be published is injured by the denial;\n\n**(2)** the officer shall publish, within **15 days** of the date, a statement of the fact of non-compliance, the reason, and the expected date of compliance, **and shall republish it every 30 days until the duty is performed**; and\n\n**(3)** the court **shall** award fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff.\n\n**(b) Ministerial stated expressly.** Where a duty requires no exercise of judgment as to whether to act, **this Act states that it is ministerial**, and no inference to the contrary may be drawn from the absence of that word in the provision creating it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 STATUTORY DUTIES AND DEADLINES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Curing the case does not end the inquiry","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 STATUTORY DUTIES AND DEADLINES","body":"**(a)** Where a proceeding has been opened on the basis of a **pattern or practice** of non-compliance, **compliance in an individual case does not terminate, moot, or excuse the proceeding as to the pattern.** The individual remedy and the compliance inquiry are separate, and relief in one does not extinguish the other."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Annual compilation","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 STATUTORY DUTIES AND DEADLINES","body":"**(a)** The Comptroller General shall publish annually a compilation of every statutory deadline not met in the preceding year, by agency, with the number of days elapsed. **This subsection creates no enforcement authority in the Comptroller General** and does not limit any action under \u00a7 7-1."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Definitions","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"In this section \u2014\n\n**(1) \"Agency action\"** has the meaning given in section 551 of title 5, United States Code.\n\n**(2) \"Federal action\"** means agency action, and any action taken by a covered official under color of federal law.\n\n**(3) \"Statement of grounds\"** means a contemporaneous written statement of the factual and legal basis for an action, identifying the authority relied upon.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Preclusion of review does not reach statutory predicates","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"No provision of law precluding or limiting judicial review of a determination shall be construed to preclude review of whether a condition, finding, consultation, or other predicate that a statute requires to be satisfied before the determination is made was in fact satisfied, unless that provision expressly so states."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Preclusion of review does not reach constitutional claims","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"No provision of law precluding or limiting judicial review shall be construed to preclude review of a claim arising under the Constitution of the United States unless that provision expressly so states."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Law to apply","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"Where a statute prescribes criteria, findings, procedures, or conditions governing an action, the action is not committed to agency discretion by law within the meaning of section 701(a)(2) of title 5, United States Code, and is reviewable for compliance with those criteria, findings, procedures, or conditions."},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Finality","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a)** An action is final for purposes of judicial review upon the earlier of its publication or the date it takes effect as to any person.\n\n**(b)** An action does not cease to be final because the agency retains authority to reconsider it, because it is styled as interim, provisional, or non-binding, or because a related proceeding remains open."},{"anchor":"sec-8-6","heading":"\u00a7 8-6. Contemporaneous grounds","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a)** A federal action may be sustained on review only upon the grounds stated by the agency or covered official at the time the action was taken.\n\n**(b)** Where no statement of grounds was made at the time the action was taken, the action may not be sustained on review, and shall be vacated or set aside without prejudice to the taking of a new action supported by a statement of grounds.\n\n**(c)** This section does not require that a statement of grounds take any particular form, and prescribes no standard of adequacy for the grounds stated."},{"anchor":"sec-8-7","heading":"\u00a7 8-7. Vacatur","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a)** The authority of a reviewing court under section 706(2) of title 5, United States Code, to set aside agency action includes the authority to vacate the action.\n\n**(b)** Vacatur under this section operates upon the action itself and is not limited in effect to the parties to the proceeding.\n\n**(c)** A court may stay the effect of a vacatur, in whole or in part, upon such terms as it considers just."},{"anchor":"sec-8-8","heading":"\u00a7 8-8. Relief extending beyond the parties","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a) Authorization.** In a civil action challenging federal action as contrary to the Constitution or as in excess of statutory authority, a court may grant relief extending beyond the parties to the action. The authority conferred by this subsection is **in addition to, and not a restatement of,** the equitable authority conferred by the Act of September 24, 1789, and by section 1651 of title 28, United States Code. That a remedy of the scope authorized here was not traditionally accorded by courts of equity does not limit this subsection.\n\n**(b) Required findings.** A court shall not grant relief under subsection (a) unless it finds, and states in the order \u2014\n\n**(1)** that relief limited to the parties cannot afford them complete relief;\n\n**(2)** that the challenged action applies uniformly, or by its terms applies to a class of persons that includes persons who are not parties; and\n\n**(3)** that the balance of equities and the public interest support relief of the scope ordered.\n\n**(b-1) Coordination with the Judiciary Integrity Act.** An order that identifies the authority conferred by subsection (a) and states the findings required by subsection (b) satisfies the statement required by \u00a7 7-5 of the Judiciary Integrity Act as to the same order, and the two need not be entered separately. Where that Act is not in force, subsection (b) operates unchanged and this subsection imposes no requirement.\n\n**(b-2) Random assignment.** A court shall not grant relief under subsection (a) unless the action was assigned to it by random selection from among all judges of the district in active service, without regard to the division or place of filing. The court shall state in the order that the action was so assigned. This subsection applies notwithstanding any local rule, standing order, or internal operating procedure of the district providing for assignment by division or by place of filing.\n\n**(1) Reassignment cures.** Where an action was not assigned as this subsection requires and relief under subsection (a) is thereafter sought \u2014 by amendment, by motion, or otherwise \u2014 the court shall direct that the action be reassigned by random selection under this subsection, and the assignment on reassignment satisfies it. An assignment made under a narrower pleading confers nothing that survives the broadening of the relief sought.\n\n**(2) Related cases.** An assignment made under a rule or order governing related, refiled, consolidated, or companion cases satisfies this subsection only if the earlier action to which the assignment refers was itself assigned as this subsection requires.\n\n**(3) Method.** Where no method of random selection has been prescribed for the district, selection shall be made by lot among all judges of the district in active service. The absence of a prescribed method does not suspend this subsection.\n\n**(4) Districts with one judge in active service.** In a district having one judge in active service, assignment of the action to that judge satisfies this subsection.\n\n**(4-1) Three-judge courts.** This subsection does not apply to an action heard by a district court of three judges convened under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 2284, which is constituted as that section prescribes.\n\n**(4-2) Courts other than district courts.** This subsection applies only to an action in a district court. Subsection (b) governs an action under subsection (a) in any other court, unchanged.\n\n**(5) Coordination with the Judiciary Integrity Act.** An assignment made as \u00a7 7-3 of the Judiciary Integrity Act prescribes satisfies this subsection. Where that Act is not in force, this subsection operates unchanged.\n\n**(6) What non-compliance does not reach.** Failure to comply with this subsection does not affect the jurisdiction of the court, the validity of any order entered, or the availability of relief limited to the parties or of any relief preserved by subsection (f). It bars relief extending beyond the parties, and only until the action is reassigned under paragraph (1).\n\n**(c) Scope.** Relief under subsection (a) shall be no broader than necessary to remedy the violation found, and the order shall state the class of persons to whom it extends.\n\n**(d) Immediate appeal.** An order granting or denying relief under subsection (a) is immediately appealable.\n\n**(e) Mandatory review.** The Supreme Court shall have jurisdiction over an appeal from an order granting relief under subsection (a), and shall decide the appeal on an expedited schedule. The Court's jurisdiction under this subsection is not discretionary.\n\n**(f) Non-exclusivity.** Nothing in this section limits vacatur under \u00a7 8-7, relief available in a class action, or any other authority to grant relief."},{"anchor":"sec-8-8a","heading":"\u00a7 8-8A. Venue for actions seeking relief beyond the parties","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a) Application.** This section governs venue in a civil action in which a party seeks relief extending beyond the parties under \u00a7 8-8. It does not govern venue in any other action. A party seeking only relief limited to the parties is unaffected by this section, and where a party withdraws a request for relief extending beyond the parties, this section ceases to apply to the action.\n\n**(b) Federal action of nationwide application.** Notwithstanding section 1391(e) of title 28, United States Code, where the challenged federal action applies throughout the United States without geographic limitation, the action shall be brought in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia.\n\n**(c) Federal action applying to a defined area.** Where the challenged federal action by its terms applies to a defined geographic area smaller than the United States, the action may be brought in any judicial district within that area, or in the District of Columbia.\n\n**(d) Federal action addressed to a particular subject.** Where the challenged federal action is addressed to a particular person, parcel, project, facility, permit, award, or place, venue lies as section 1391 of title 28, United States Code, provides, and subsections (b) and (c) do not apply.\n\n**(e) How the lane is determined.** The applicable subsection is determined from the terms of the challenged federal action itself, and not from the scope of the relief requested, the residence of any party, or the reach of the injury alleged. Where a complaint challenges federal actions falling in more than one of subsections (b) through (d), venue proper for the action of broadest application is proper for the whole.\n\n**(f) Improper venue is cured by transfer, never by dismissal.** Where an action to which this section applies is filed in a district not proper under it, the court shall transfer the action under section 1406(a) of title 28, United States Code, to a district in which it could have been brought. The court shall not dismiss the action for improper venue under this section, and shall not deny relief limited to the parties by reason of it.\n\n**(g) What this section does not do.** This section does not confer jurisdiction, does not enlarge or restrict the relief a court may grant, does not alter section 1404 of title 28, United States Code, and does not apply to an action heard by a district court of three judges convened under section 2284 of that title."},{"anchor":"sec-8-8b","heading":"\u00a7 8-8B. Three-judge court and direct appeal for constitutional challenges","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a) Application; how an Act elects it.** This section applies to a civil action for declaratory or injunctive relief challenging the constitutionality of a provision of an Act of Congress **that provides that this section applies to it.** No other action is within this section, and an Act that does not so provide is unaffected by it.\n\n**(b) The channel.** In an action to which this section applies and that is commenced within the period stated in subsection (f) \u2014\n\n**(1)** the action shall be heard by a district court of three judges convened under section 2284 of title 28, United States Code, which is required to be convened by this paragraph within the meaning of subsection (a) of that section;\n\n**(2) Direct appeal, on the period existing law already sets.** A final decision in the action, and an order granting or denying an interlocutory or permanent injunction in it, are reviewable only by appeal directly to the Supreme Court of the United States. **This paragraph is a provision otherwise made by law within the meaning of section 1253 of title 28, United States Code, and reaches a final decision whether or not that decision grants or denies an injunction**, which section 1253 alone does not. An appeal under this paragraph is taken within the period prescribed by section 2101 of that title, and the record is made up and the case docketed as subsection (a) of that section provides;\n\n**(2-A) The period is not shortened.** This section does not abbreviate the time to appeal.\n\n**(3)** it is the duty of the three-judge court and of the Supreme Court to advance the action on the docket and to expedite its disposition to the greatest possible extent; and\n\n**(4)** no stay of an order entered in the action is issued by a single Justice of the Supreme Court.\n\n**(c) Ripeness is not a reason to wait.** Where the provision challenged imposes a duty, prohibition, or condition that operates upon a person by force of its enactment, that person's subjection to it is an injury at the time it operates, and \u2014\n\n**(1)** no exhaustion of any administrative remedy is a prerequisite to an action brought by **that person**;\n\n**(2)** the provision is final as to that person when it operates, and neither the availability of reconsideration, guidance, waiver, or rulemaking, nor the absence of any enforcement action, makes **that person's** challenge unripe or premature; and\n\n**(3)** an assertion by the United States of a want of ripeness, a want of finality, or a failure to exhaust, in an action brought by **that person**, **is of no effect**, and no court shall dismiss such an action upon any of those grounds. **The waiver operates by force of this paragraph and does not depend on the United States declining to raise the point.**\n\n**(c-0) The waiver runs with the duty, and no further.** Subsection (c) reaches **only a plaintiff on whom the provision challenged itself operates**, and every paragraph of it is limited to an action brought by such a plaintiff. **A plaintiff who is not subject to the provision \u2014 including a State or political subdivision suing on account of the provision's effects on its residents, its revenues, or its own programs rather than on account of a duty the provision places upon it \u2014 takes nothing from subsection (c)**, and ripeness, finality and exhaustion apply to that plaintiff as they would in any other action. Nothing in this subsection affects who may use the procedure under subsection (e).\n\n**(c-1) What subsection (c) does not do.** Subsection (c) waives requirements that are not imposed by Article III of the Constitution. **It does not confer standing, does not dispense with injury in fact, causation, or redressability, and creates no cause of action.** A plaintiff who does not satisfy Article III is not aided by it.\n\n**(d) A defendant who can be sued.** In an action under this section the officer charged with administering the provision challenged, and the United States, are proper defendants, and the action shall not be dismissed on the ground that no officer administers the provision or that no officer has threatened to enforce it. **Where a provision is enforceable only by a person who is not an officer of the United States, the officer to whom it assigns any receipt, publication, registration, certification, or record-keeping function is a proper defendant**, and where it assigns none, the Attorney General is.\n\n**(e) Who may use the channel, and who is not confined by it.** The procedure of subsection (b) is available to a person subject to the provision challenged, a person who will become subject to it, a State, and a political subdivision of a State. **This subsection enumerates who may invoke the procedure of this section. It does not enumerate who may bring an action, and no person's right to challenge the constitutionality of any provision, in any court otherwise open to that person, is limited by this section or by an omission from this subsection.**\n\n**(f) The period.** The period referred to in subsection (b) is the period ending on the date stated in the schedule to this Act, measured from the date the provision challenged takes effect as to any person.\n\n**(g) After the period: the action is channeled, never barred.** An action to which this section applies that is commenced after the period stated in subsection (f) proceeds as any other civil action, and \u2014\n\n**(1)** a judgment entered under subsection (b) operates as precedent according to its terms, and **does not preclude a person who was not a party to it from challenging the same provision;**\n\n**(2) the channel remains available by election.** Any party, including the United States, may elect the procedure of subsection (b) by filing a notice of election within 30 days of the filing of the complaint, and upon that election subsection (b) governs the action;\n\n**(3)** where no election is made under paragraph (2), the district court may, on the motion of a party and upon a determination that the question is substantial and that its resolution will materially advance the disposition of the action, certify a question of the constitutionality of the provision to the court of appeals for the circuit, which shall hear the question sitting en banc. **A motion under this paragraph does not stay proceedings**, and the court may deny it without opinion; and\n\n**(4)** nothing in this section shortens, extends, or otherwise governs any period of limitation.\n\n**(h) Intervention by Members of Congress.** In any action in which the constitutionality of a provision to which this section applies is raised, including an action described in subsection (b), any Member of the House of Representatives, including a Delegate or Resident Commissioner to the Congress, or of the Senate, **has the right to intervene either in support of or in opposition to the position of a party** regarding the constitutionality of the provision. **The right conferred by this subsection is unconditional within the meaning of Rule 24(a)(1) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure.** **An intervenor under this subsection seeks no relief other than that already sought by a party**, and this subsection confers no standing and dispenses with no requirement of Article III as to any relief an intervenor may separately seek.\n\n**(h-1) The court manages the intervention.** The court may require Members intervening under subsection (h) who take the same position to appear jointly, to file a single brief, and to share the time allowed for argument, and may set the length and timing of any filing. **This subsection governs the form of participation and does not condition, limit, or deny the right conferred by subsection (h).**\n\n**(i) Notice to Congress.** A copy of a complaint in an action under this section shall be delivered promptly to the Clerk of the House of Representatives and the Secretary of the Senate. **Section 2403(a) of title 28, United States Code, requires certification to the Attorney General where the United States is not a party; this subsection is in addition to it, reaches Congress rather than the Attorney General, and neither displaces nor satisfies it.**\n\n**(i-1) One route to the Supreme Court, not two.** An action may both be within this section and seek relief extending beyond the parties under \u00a7 8-8, and each provides for review by the Supreme Court that is not discretionary. **Where an action is governed by subsection (b) of this section, review of a final decision in it is by direct appeal under subsection (b)(2), and \u00a7 8-8(e) does not separately apply to that decision.** An order granting or denying relief under \u00a7 8-8 that is entered before final decision remains immediately appealable under \u00a7 8-8(d), and that appeal lies to the Supreme Court under \u00a7 8-8(e). **Neither provision is a ground for declining review under the other**, and where the application of this subsection is uncertain, review lies under whichever provision was first invoked.\n\n**(j) What this section does not do.** This section prescribes forum, procedure, and the timing of review. **It states no standard of decision, prescribes no outcome, and does not enlarge or restrict the relief a court may grant.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-8c","heading":"\u00a7 8-8C. Stay pending review in an action against the United States","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(a) Application.** This section applies where a stay pending appeal or pending review is sought in a civil action against the United States, an agency of the United States, or an officer of the United States acting in an official capacity, **including relief pending review under section 705 of title 5, United States Code, and a stay sought under section 1651 or section 2101(f) of title 28, United States Code, from any court of the United States, including the Supreme Court or a Justice of it.** Section 705 authorizes that relief to the extent necessary to prevent irreparable injury and states no further standard governing it. **Subsections (a-1) and (a-2) state a presumption governing the stays they describe, which applies notwithstanding any other provision of law and any rule of court, whenever prescribed, and the remainder of this section requires that the court say what it found.** The second sentence of section 2072(b) of title 28, United States Code, does not apply to any provision of this section. In this section, \"stay\" includes an injunction pending appeal or pending review.\n\n**(a-1) Presumption against a stay that alters the status quo.**\n\n**(1) The presumption.** A stay that would alter the status quo, or that would afford the applicant relief it has not obtained on the merits within the meaning of subsection (a-2)(2), is presumed not to be warranted. The presumption is overcome only where the court finds that each of the four matters stated in subsection (b) favors the stay, and that the injury the applicant would suffer absent the stay is greater than the injury the stay would cause the other parties and the public.\n\n**(2) The status quo.** In this section, the status quo is \u2014\n\n**(A)** where the action challenges an action or conduct of the United States, an agency, or an officer, the condition that obtained immediately before that action or conduct took effect as to a party to the action, unless, when the first challenge to it in any court was commenced, it had been in effect as to that party for not less than the period set in the schedule to this Act; and\n\n**(B)** in every other case, including the case excepted from subparagraph (A), the condition that obtained when the action was commenced.\n\n**(3) What is not the status quo.** No order entered in the action or in any other action, and nothing done under or during such an order, forms part of the status quo.\n\n**(4) Alteration.** A stay alters the status quo where the condition that would obtain while it is in effect differs from the status quo in a respect at issue in the action.\n\n**(5) The statement.** The statement required by subsection (b) shall identify the status quo as this subsection defines it, and whether the stay would alter it.\n\n**(a-2) Presumption against relief not obtained on the merits.**\n\n**(1) The presumption.** A stay that would afford the applicant relief it has not obtained on the merits is presumed not to be warranted. The presumption is overcome only where the court finds that each of the four matters stated in subsection (b) favors the stay, and that the injury the applicant would suffer absent the stay is greater than the injury the stay would cause the other parties and the public.\n\n**(2) Relief not obtained on the merits.** A stay affords the applicant relief it has not obtained on the merits where, while the stay is in effect, it would give the applicant the substance of a result that a court in the action, after considering the merits, has refused the applicant or ordered against it, whether by a final judgment or in deciding an application for preliminary or interim relief, and that no later decision in the action on the merits has given the applicant.\n\n**(3) In force on its own terms.** This subsection operates from the effective date of this Act whether or not subsection (a-1) is in effect, and is not conditioned on any holding concerning subsection (a-1).\n\n**(a-3) What the presumptions do not do.** The presumptions of subsections (a-1) and (a-2) arise from the effect of the stay sought and not from the identity of any party, and apply in the same terms to every applicant, including the United States. They shift no burden to the party opposing the stay and do not displace the four matters stated in subsection (b). A stay that would neither alter the status quo nor afford relief not obtained on the merits is not subject to either presumption. Overcoming a presumption is a condition of a stay and never a ground for one, and nothing in this section authorizes relief that another provision of law forbids or limits.\n\n**(b) Findings on the record.** A court granting or denying a stay to which this section applies shall state on the record its findings on each of \u2014 **(1)** whether the applicant has made a strong showing that the applicant is likely to succeed on the merits; **(2)** whether the applicant will be irreparably injured absent a stay; **(3)** whether issuance of the stay will substantially injure the other parties interested in the proceeding; and **(4)** where the public interest lies.\n\n**(c) Subjects, not content.** Subsection (b) prescribes the subjects on which findings are stated. **Apart from the presumptions of subsections (a-1) and (a-2), this section prescribes no standard, assigns no weight to any factor, and does not direct the disposition of any application.** The burden of showing that the circumstances justify a stay remains on the party requesting it.\n\n**(d) The status quo, identified.** A statement under subsection (b) shall identify **what the stay would suspend and what condition would obtain while it is in effect.** An order that alters the status quo, or that affords the applicant relief it has not obtained on the merits, shall be identified as such in the statement.\n\n**(e) Effect of the statement.** An order entered without the statement required by this section is effective according to its terms, and the absence of a statement is not a ground for reversal, for vacatur, or for any collateral attack upon the order.\n\n**(e-1) The absence is entered, not presumed.** Where an order to which this section applies is entered without the statement required by subsection (b), **the clerk shall enter on the docket that the order was entered without it.** The entry is ministerial, states no view of the order, and is the only consequence of the omission.\n\n**(e-2) Review of the presumptions.** Subsections (e) and (e-1) govern the consequence of omitting the statement and nothing else. They do not limit review, in the ordinary course, of whether a presumption under subsection (a-1) or (a-2) was overcome.\n\n**(f) Severability within this section.**\n\n**(1) The presumptions.** Subsection (a-1) is severable from subsection (a-2), and each of them is severable from subsections (b) through (e-2). If subsection (a-1) is held invalid, subsection (a-2) continues in effect; if subsection (a-2) is also held invalid, subsections (b) through (e-2) continue in effect, and the exception for the presumptions in subsection (c) has no operation. Congress would have enacted subsection (a-2) without subsection (a-1), and subsections (b) through (e-2) without either; the three are not one scheme. A holding that the presumption of subsection (a-2) is invalid does not affect the use of paragraph (2) of that subsection as a definition for subsection (a-1). A holding that the presumption of subsection (a-1) is invalid does not affect the use of paragraphs (2) through (4) of that subsection as definitions for subsection (d).\n\n**(2) By court.** If subsection (a-1) or (a-2) is held invalid as applied to a stay sought from the Supreme Court or a Justice of it, it continues in effect as applied to a stay sought from every other court. If it is held invalid as applied to a stay sought from a court of appeals, it continues in effect as applied to a stay sought from a district court.\n\n**(3) The statement.** Subsections (b), (c), (e) and (e-1), which require and govern a statement of findings, are severable from subsection (d), which addresses what the statement shall identify about the status quo. If subsection (d) is held invalid, the remainder of this section continues in effect, and the reverse.\n\n**(g) Relation to other provisions.**\n\n**(1) Within this Act.** A stay of a vacatur under \u00a7 8-7(c) is a stay to which this section applies where subsection (a) describes it. A stay of the operation of a covered duty described in \u00a7 0-4(c)(2) and (g)(4) is a stay to which this section applies in any action, whoever brings it and whether the stay is sought pending appeal or pending adjudication. In each case the authority those provisions preserve to act upon such terms as the court considers just is exercised subject to this section.\n\n**(2) The Enforceable Rights Act.** In an action under the Enforceable Rights Act, \u00a7 7-4 of that Act applies according to its terms, and this section applies in addition to it. The presumptions of subsections (a-1) and (a-2) arise from the effect of the stay sought and are not a presumption by reason of the identity of a party within the meaning of \u00a7 7-4(e) of that Act.\n\n**(3) The Judiciary Integrity Act.** Sections 8-4A(c) and 8-4B(c) of the Judiciary Integrity Act each reserve as to that section only, and neither limits this section. Where that Act is not in force, this section operates unchanged."},{"anchor":"sec-8-9","heading":"\u00a7 8-9. Forum for non-monetary relief","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"Where a plaintiff seeks relief other than money damages with respect to the termination, suspension, or modification of a grant, cooperative agreement, or other award, the claim arises under chapter 7 of title 5, United States Code, and the waiver of sovereign immunity in section 702 of that title applies. Such a claim is not, by reason of the award's contractual character, within the exclusive jurisdiction conferred by section 1491 of title 28, United States Code."},{"anchor":"sec-8-10","heading":"\u00a7 8-10. Rules of construction","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"**(1) No jurisdiction conferred except as stated.** Except as expressly provided in \u00a7 8-8(a), \u00a7 8-8(e), \u00a7 8-8B(b), and \u00a7 8-9, this section confers no jurisdiction and enlarges no tribunal's authority.\n\n**(2) No standard of decision prescribed.** Except as \u00a7 8-8C(a-1) and (a-2) provide, nothing in this section directs the grounds upon which any court decides any question, or the outcome of any proceeding.\n\n**(3) No new cause of action.** Except as expressly provided, this section creates no right of action and no remedy not otherwise available.\n\n**(4) Existing review preserved.** Nothing in this section limits any authority to obtain review that exists apart from it."},{"anchor":"sec-8-11","heading":"\u00a7 8-11. Severability","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW AND REMEDIES","body":"Each section within this Section is severable from every other, and from every other provision of this Act. If any provision is held invalid, or invalid as applied, the remainder continues in effect. Congress would have enacted each of \u00a7\u00a7 8-2 through 8-9, including \u00a7 8-8A, \u00a7 8-8B, and \u00a7 8-8C, independently of any other. **In particular, \u00a7 8-8B is severable from \u00a7 8-8 and from \u00a7 8-8A: a holding that relief extending beyond the parties may not be authorized as \u00a7 8-8 provides, or that venue may not be fixed as \u00a7 8-8A provides, does not reach the forum, procedure, and timing of review that \u00a7 8-8B prescribes, and the reverse.** Within \u00a7 8-8B, subsection (c) is severable from subsection (b), so that a holding that a requirement waived by subsection (c) may not be waived leaves the channel of subsection (b) in force."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Construction and procedure","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, PROCEDURE, AND SEVERABILITY","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses and limitations available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. **No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act**, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense recognized at common law is a determination by Congress that it is not available.\n\n**(b) Pleading.** A complaint alleging that an individualized adverse action was taken, and that the determination required by \u00a7 2-2 was not made or does not satisfy that Section, states a claim. **No heightened pleading requirement applies.** Departure may be alleged generally and proved by the counts published under \u00a7 1-3, the agency's records, and circumstantial evidence.\n\n**(c) No exhaustion; no channeling.** No administrative remedy is a prerequisite to an action under this Act. **No statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to channel, preclude, or displace a claim under this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act.**\n\n**(d) Mootness.** The rescission or expiration of an individualized adverse action after the commencement of an action does not moot it where the agency remains free to take the action again, and **the agency bears the burden** of establishing that it cannot reasonably be expected to do so.\n\n**(e) Production.** Where the determination required by \u00a7 2-2 is within the agency's control, **a claim may not be dismissed before the plaintiff has had a reasonable opportunity to obtain it.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 9 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, PROCEDURE, AND SEVERABILITY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Severability","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, PROCEDURE, AND SEVERABILITY","body":"**(a) Section 1 stands alone.** Section 1 imposes duties that operate **without regard to whether any action is taken against any person**, and its operation does not depend on the validity of Section 2. **Congress would have enacted Section 1 independently of every other Section of this Act**, and if any provision of any other Section is held invalid, Section 1 continues in full effect.\n\n**(b) Section 2 without Section 1.** If any provision of Section 1 is held invalid, **\u00a7 2-2 continues in effect** according to its terms, the determination requirement being independent of any record of practice. **\u00a7 2-3 continues in effect** to the extent departure can be established from the agency's records under \u00a7 2-1(b).\n\n**(c) Every Section, and within each.** Each Section of this Act is severable from each other Section, subsections (a) and (b) stating the particulars for Sections 1 and 2. Within each Section, each section is severable from each other section, and each subsection from each other subsection."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Anti-substitution","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, PROCEDURE, AND SEVERABILITY","body":"**(a) Statement required.** Where a covered authority has been held unavailable, or has been determined not to authorise an individualized adverse action, and an action of substantially the same character against substantially the same person is then taken on the basis of another authority \u2014 **including an authority asserted under Article II of the Constitution** \u2014 the agency shall publish a statement identifying:\n1. the covered authority previously relied upon;\n2. the holding or determination;\n3. the authority now asserted, and the provision of it relied upon; and\n4. **the material differences in the conditions each imposes.**\n\n**(b) Timing.** The statement shall be published **before the action takes effect**, and in any event within 30 days of the holding or determination.\n\n**(c) Substituted covered authority.** Where the substituted authority is itself a covered authority, **Section 2 applies to it according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) No inference.** Nothing in this Section confers, limits, or construes any authority under Article II of the Constitution. **It imposes a publication duty and nothing else**, and that duty is ministerial and enforceable as provided in \u00a7 1-4."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Period for a departing interpretation's baseline** (\u00a7 5-4(a-1)): [**10 years**]\n2. **Retention period for a covered record** (\u00a7 6-1(b)): [___ years from the making of the record, or until the matter is closed, whichever is later]\n3. **Outer limit on the retention period** (\u00a7 6-1(b)): [___ years]\n4. **Period a preservation demand remains effective before it lapses** (\u00a7 6-4(c)): [___ days]\n5. **Threshold above which a class of case is high-volume** (\u00a7 2-2(f-1)): [___ determinations per year]\n6. **Period to furnish a corrected determination** (\u00a7 2-2(f-1)(2)): [___ days]\n7. **Default determination time where none is published** (\u00a7 2-2A(e)): [___ days]\n8. **Period to make a constitutional assertion where performance was due before it could reasonably be formulated** (\u00a7 0-4(e)(4)): [___ days after performance was due]\n9. **Period to publish a constitutional assertion and transmit it to the Judiciary Committees** (\u00a7 0-4(e)(5)): [___ days after it is made]\n10. **Period to commence a declaratory action or withdraw before an assertion lapses** (\u00a7 0-4(e)(7)): [___ days after the assertion is made]\n11. **Period within which the effectiveness of a conditioned action may be raised** (\u00a7 0-4(f)(3)): [___ after the action was taken or the omission became known]\n12. **Window after protected conduct within which an adverse action against an officer requires a published statement of grounds** (\u00a7 0-4(h)(3)): [___ days]\n13. **Period for initial designation of officer positions** (\u00a7 0-6(b)(1)): [___ days after the effective date]\n14. **Period for designation after a position is established or materially altered** (\u00a7 0-6(b)(1)): [___ days]\n15. **Period to furnish the writing recording a covered order on request** (\u00a7 4-2(c)): [___ hours after the request]\n16. **Period to furnish the writing where exigency required action first** (\u00a7 4-2(c)): [___ hours after the action is carried out]\n17. **Window after protected conduct within which an adverse action against a directed officer requires a published statement of grounds** (\u00a7 4-4(c)): [___ days]\n18. **Period to publish that a requested record was not made** (\u00a7 4-5(c)): [___ days]\n20. **Outer bound on a published determination time** (\u00a7 2-2A(b-1)): [___ days]\n21. **Share of determinations made after the published time that constitutes a pattern** (\u00a7 2-2A(h-1)): [___ percent of a class of case in a year]\n22. **Share of investigations whose findings are published after 180 days that constitutes a pattern** (\u00a7 3-2(f-2)): [___ percent in a year]\n23. **Share of determinations conforming to an automated system's output that constitutes a pattern** (\u00a7 2-2B(f)): [___ percent of a class of case in a year]\n19. **Period within which an action gets the three-judge channel** (\u00a7 8-8B(f)): [___ days after the provision takes effect as to any person]\n24. **Period of unchallenged effect after which an action's operation is the status quo** (\u00a7 8-8C(a-1)(2)(A)): [___ months in effect before the first challenge]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-10"}}}};
